00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.573
Let's talk about sleep and
tools to optimize your sleep.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.380
I want you to conceptualize yourself

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.350
as contained within a room

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:39.330
that has only very few windows
or very few entry points.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.163
What do I mean by this?

00:08:40.163 --> 00:08:44.070
Well, your brain and your
nervous system control

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.930
whether or not you move or don't move.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.820
They control whether or
not you're digesting food

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.890
or you're not digesting food.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.690
They control whether or not
you're stressed or not stressed,

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.550
happy or sad, et cetera.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:57.030
All of that stuff that
controls all that stuff

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:00.780
is housed inside your
skin and skull, et cetera.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.520
That might seem pretty obvious,

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.480
but what that means is that
for your brain and body

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.660
to feel alert and focused,

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:12.960
ready to move and
exercise or do some work,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.310
or if your brain body are going
to lie down and go to sleep,

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:22.290
well, that brain and body
needs cues, it needs inputs,

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.770
to determine when to do
those different things.

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.170
And those cues and inputs arrive

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:31.080
through a defined set of
what I'll call stimuli,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.869
but you can also think of
these as levers or tools.

00:09:33.869 --> 00:09:37.260
The main levers and tools
that are going to allow you

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.590
to control when you are
awake and when you are asleep

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:43.530
and to get better sleep every single night

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:48.060
are light, literally
photons, light energy,

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.980
could be from sunlight, could
be from artificial light,

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.920
we will discuss those
particulars in a moment,

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.110
as well as darkness.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:57.840
That is the absence of light.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:59.340
So we've got light and dark.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.220
Those are two very powerful tools

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.600
to encourage your nervous system

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.130
to be in one state or another,

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:06.393
meaning awake or asleep.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:11.040
Temperature is another tool or lever.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.250
Turns out that when your
body is cooling down,

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:18.090
you have a greater tendency
to fall and stay asleep.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:21.150
In fact, every night
when you actually sleep,

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:23.520
your body is dropping
by one to three degrees

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.580
and that drop in temperature is required.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.130
It's like a gate that your
body has to go through

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.740
in order for you to get into sleep.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.690
And in fact, the converse is also true.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:37.050
If your body heats up by
one to three degrees or so,

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.460
you will wake up.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:43.460
So you've got light,
dark, temperature, food.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.210
And when we say food,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.810
we mean what we eat, when we eat,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.120
and the amount that we eat.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.360
Okay, so light, dark,
temperature, food, exercise.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.700
And of course, exercise
comes in different forms.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.350
We can do cardiovascular exercise

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:01.890
that can be low-intensity,
long-distance exercise.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.450
It can be high intensity,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.460
so-called high intensity
interval training.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:07.560
It could be weight
training. It could be yoga.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.870
It could be swimming, any
number of different activities.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.270
But exercise, in general, causes

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.760
an increase in body temperature

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.280
and tends to make us more alert,

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.810
not just during the exercise,

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.870
but in the immediate
hours after that exercise.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:23.160
Exercise does some other things

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.390
that relate to our sleep as well

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:25.680
and we'll talk about those today

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.090
and how you can leverage them.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:28.680
Another potent lever

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:33.680
for adjusting your sleepiness
and wakefulness is caffeine.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.360
This, of course, comes
as no surprise to people,

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:40.800
but why and how caffeine works
might come as a surprise.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.830
Very briefly, we have a molecule

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.390
in our body called adenosine

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.490
and the longer we have been awake,

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:49.800
the more adenosine builds
up in our brain and body

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.740
and adenosine is part of the
reason why we get sleepy.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:57.740
Caffeine effectively operates
as a adenosine antagonist.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:01.560
It works by basically occupying
the receptor for adenosine.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.840
So it's a little bit of
a convoluted mechanism.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:05.550
But basically all you need to know

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.670
is that caffeine prevents
the actions of adenosine.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.520
That's one of the reasons why
caffeine makes us feel alert.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:12.900
But how much caffeine we drink

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:15.390
and when we drink caffeine turns
out to be vitally important

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:17.490
for adjusting our wakefulness

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:18.810
and for optimizing our sleep.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:20.100
So we'll talk about that as well.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:21.810
The other category of lever or tools

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:24.990
which are immensely powerful
for optimizing sleep

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.790
are supplements.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.910
There now exist as many as
eight different supplements

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:33.390
that can powerfully modulate
sleep in healthy ways

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.850
and that have huge margins for safety.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.340
So we're going to talk about
what those supplements are.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.170
In previous episodes of this podcast

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.450
and as a guest on other podcasts,

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:44.430
I've talked about three
particular supplements,

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:48.540
magnesium threonate,
apigenin, and theanine,

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.220
which together can really enhance

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.290
the speed at which one falls asleep

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.360
and people's ability to stay asleep

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.373
and to really get into
those deep stages of sleep

00:12:56.373 --> 00:12:58.200
that are particularly restorative.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.820
Today we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:00.870
about each of those three

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:03.240
and how they can best
be used in combination,

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.880
but we are also going to touch
on some other supplements

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:09.150
that I have not talked about
much before, if at all.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:13.170
Things like glycine and
GABA, as well as inositol.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.690
Many people are going to
find inositol interesting

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:18.180
and of particular use to them,

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.880
especially if they're following
a low-carbohydrate diet

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.480
or if they are fasting before sleep

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:26.430
or just trying to avoid
eating too close to bedtime

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.190
and yet they're having a
hard time falling asleep.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.560
Inositol also turns out
to be especially useful

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.610
for people who have a tendency

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.470
to wake up in the middle of the night

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.200
and have a hard time falling back asleep.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.850
It also has some interesting
and potent effects

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:40.200
on anxiety throughout the day.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.590
So we're going to talk about
inositol as a tool as well.

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.720
And then last in our list
of general categories

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.360
of levers and tools for optimizing sleep

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:49.327
are digital tools.

00:13:49.327 --> 00:13:51.270
Now, when we say digital tools,

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:52.710
I don't necessarily mean devices.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:56.220
What I mean are things like
non-sleep deep rest scripts.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:59.220
These are zero-cost
scripts that you listen to

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.350
that take your body through
some deep relaxation

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:05.010
and that can help people both
fall asleep, stay asleep,

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.890
fall back asleep, and
get better at sleeping.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.380
And also going to talk about digital tools

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:11.820
related to self-hypnosis.

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:14.220
This is distinctly different
from stage hypnosis.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.080
So I know some of you hear
hypnosis and you think,

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.960
oh, you know, people
clucking like chickens

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.030
and doing things that are
outside their control.

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:22.650
That's not at all what
I'm referring to here.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:26.670
I'm talking about clinically
and research-supported tools

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:29.550
that have been shown to
enhance people's ability

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:30.780
to fall and stay asleep

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:34.170
and that can get you
far better at sleeping.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:36.930
So again, to recap the
list of levers and tools,

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:38.787
we've got light and dark,

00:14:38.787 --> 00:14:40.530
and that includes the intensity of light,

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:41.700
the timing of light, et cetera.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:42.900
We've got temperature.

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:44.010
We have food.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:49.010
We have exercise, caffeine,
supplements, and digital tools,

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:50.700
not just limited to devices,

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:54.210
but zero-cost tools that
you can access on YouTube

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:55.830
and elsewhere in various apps

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:57.630
that can really help
you optimize your sleep.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.250
So today we're going to
talk about all of these.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.710
I really want to provide you
as many tools as possible,

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.450
give you the logic behind
each of those tools

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:05.640
and when and how best to apply them

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.520
so that you can develop the sleep toolkit

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.650
that's ideal for your sleep needs.

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:12.240
As we head into our description

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.550
of tools for optimizing sleep,

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:18.390
let's consider what the
perfect 24-hour cycle

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:19.920
would look like.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:21.570
Let's start this 24-hour cycle

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.340
with when you wake up in the morning.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.350
So for some of you, that will be 5:00 a.m.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:27.360
For others of you, that will be 10:00 a.m.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:28.620
Most people, I believe, wake up

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:32.700
sometime between 6:30 a.m. and 8:30 a.m.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:35.310
But regardless of when you
wake up in the morning,

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:36.930
one of the first things that happens

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.780
is that your body
temperature is increasing

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.790
and that's just going to happen naturally.

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:43.410
Some of it is going to be the consequence

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:45.000
of your moving around a bit,

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.460
but really the increase
in body temperature

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.050
is one of the main triggers

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.183
for why you woke up in the first place.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:56.250
That increase in body temperature
in turn causes an increase

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.980
in the release of a
hormone called cortisol.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.890
Cortisol is often discussed
as a stress hormone,

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:03.900
but it's not just associated with stress.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:06.510
It also enhances your immune system

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.780
provided cortisol is
elevated at the right times,

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.700
and the right time for
cortisol to be elevated

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:13.953
is when you first wake up in the morning.

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:15.960
That increase in cortisol

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.930
is also going to increase metabolism.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:22.500
It's also going to increase
your ability to focus mentally

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.750
and for you to move your body.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.870
So again, cortisol is often demonized

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.130
and consider this bad thing.

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:30.750
And indeed, you don't want cortisol

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:33.210
to be chronically or consistently elevated

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:34.710
throughout the day or night.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:38.400
But you do want cortisol to
reach its peak early in the day

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.860
right about the time you wake up.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.510
One way that you can ensure

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:45.030
that that cortisol peak
occurs early in the day

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:47.100
right about the time that you wake up

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.790
is to view bright light,
ideally from sunlight,

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:54.303
within the first 30 to
60 minutes after waking.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.810
That's right, view bright sunlight

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:01.860
within the first 30 to
60 minutes after waking.

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:03.270
I'll get into all the caveats

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:05.520
about what happens if you
wake up before the sun is out,

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.440
what if you live in the
UK where there is no sun,

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:08.610
or people claim there is no sun.

00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:11.782
Hate to tell you this, folks,
but there is sun in the UK.

00:17:11.782 --> 00:17:13.290
We'll talk about all that.

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:17.280
But everybody, whether or not
you live in a cloudy place

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:18.390
or a sunny place,

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.970
whether or not there's
cloud cover or not that day,

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:24.090
should really strive to get
bright light in your eyes,

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:25.500
ideally from sunlight,

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:29.160
within the first 30 to
60 minutes after waking.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:31.590
The reason for that is very simple.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:33.660
You want to trigger that cortisol increase

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:37.050
to occur very early in your day,

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:39.540
and you don't want that
cortisol peak to happen later,

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:40.740
which is what will happen

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.623
if you wait to get
outside and see sunlight.

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:45.990
The reason for this

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:49.350
is that you have a set of
neurons, nerve cells, in your eye.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:50.910
They're called
intrinsically photosensitive

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:51.743
melanopsin cells,

00:17:51.743 --> 00:17:53.550
but you do not need to know that name.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:56.430
Those neurons respond
best to bright light,

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.730
and especially right after
waking early in the day,

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:02.760
they are best able to
signal to a set of neurons

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.710
that reside over the roof of your mouth

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.180
called the suprachiasmatic nucleus,

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:07.170
which is a cluster of neurons

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:10.050
that then sends a huge
number of other signals,

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:11.040
electrical and chemical,

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.540
out to your entire body

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.970
that triggers that cortisol increase,

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:16.980
provides a wake-up signal
for your brain and body,

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:19.320
and sets in motion a timer

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:21.450
for you to fall asleep later that night.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:22.920
So again, we're not trying to go into

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:24.000
too much mechanism today.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.830
We are trying to really hammer on tools

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:27.690
and I'll substantiate those tools

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:29.550
just a bit with some mechanism.

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:31.890
But here's what you do, or
at least here's what I do.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.896
I wake up in the morning and
I want to reach for my phone,

00:18:34.896 --> 00:18:37.590
but I know that even if I were
to crank up the brightness

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:38.820
on that phone screen,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:42.120
it's not bright enough to
trigger that cortisol spike

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:45.930
and for me to be at my
most alert and focused

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:46.763
throughout the day

00:18:46.763 --> 00:18:48.000
and to optimize my sleep at night.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.870
So what I do is I get out
of bed and I go outside.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:55.200
And if it's a bright, clear day

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.030
and the sun is low in the sky

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.040
or the sun is, you know,
starting to get overhead,

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.570
what we call low solar angle,

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:03.633
then I know I'm getting
outside at the right time.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.160
If there's cloud cover
and I can't see the sun,

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:09.750
I also know I'm doing a good thing

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:11.940
because it turns out,
especially on cloudy days,

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:12.780
you want to get outside

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:15.780
and get as much light energy
or photons in your eyes.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.670
But let's say it's a very clear day

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.830
and I can see where the sun is.

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.590
I do not need to stare
directly into the sun.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:25.590
If it's very low in the
sky, I might do that

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.900
because it's not going to
be very painful to my eyes.

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.043
However, if the sun is
a little bit brighter

00:19:30.043 --> 00:19:31.890
and a little bit higher in the sky,

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:33.570
sometimes it could be painful to look at.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.290
So the way to get this sunlight
viewing early in the day

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.990
is to look toward the sun.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.330
If it's too bright to look at directly,

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:43.290
well, then don't do that.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.570
You just look toward it,
but not directly at it.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.730
It's absolutely fine to blink.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:49.020
In fact, I encourage you to blink

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:51.450
whenever you feel the impulse to blink.

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:54.240
Never look at any light,
sunlight or otherwise,

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:55.860
that's so bright that
it's painful to look at

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:57.510
'cause you can damage your eyes.

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.850
But for this morning sunlight viewing,

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.890
it's best to not wear sunglasses,

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:04.260
that's right, to not wear sunglasses,

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.990
at least for this
morning sunlight viewing.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:11.640
It is absolutely fine to wear
eyeglasses or contact lenses,

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:12.750
so-called corrective lenses.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.850
In fact, those will serve you well

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.440
in this practice or this tool

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.600
because they will focus the
light onto your neural retina

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:19.590
and onto those melanopsin

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:21.750
intrinsically photosensitive
ganglion cells.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:24.000
If your eyeglasses or contact lenses

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:27.300
have UV protection, that's okay.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:28.740
There's so many different
wavelengths of light

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:30.330
coming from the sun

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:31.410
and they are bright enough

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:33.120
that they will trigger the mechanisms

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:36.900
that you want triggered
at this early time of day.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:38.580
So try and get outside,

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.440
ideally within the first
five minutes of waking

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.240
or maybe it's 15 minutes,

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:44.790
but certainly within the
first hour after waking.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:46.950
I want to share with you
three critical things

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:49.260
about this tool of
morning sunlight viewing.

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:53.040
First of all, this is not
some woo biology thing.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.980
This is grounded in the
core of our physiology.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:58.110
There are literally
hundreds, if not thousands,

00:20:58.110 --> 00:20:59.550
of quality peer-reviewed papers

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:03.030
showing that light
viewing early in the day

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.740
is the most powerful stimulus

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:06.240
for wakefulness throughout the day

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.850
and it has a powerful, positive impact

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:10.950
on your ability to fall
and stay asleep at night.

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.920
So this is really the
foundational power tool

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:16.530
for ensuring a great night's sleep

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:19.080
and for feeling more awake during the day.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:23.613
Second of all, if you wake
up before the sun is out,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:28.350
you can, and probably should,
flip on artificial lights

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:30.750
in your internal home
environment or apartment

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:32.130
or wherever you happen to live

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:34.020
if your goal is to be awake, right?

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:35.100
If you wake up at four in the morning

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:36.000
and you need to be awake,

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:38.490
well, then turn on artificial lights.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:40.410
Once the sun is out, however,

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:42.600
once the sun has risen,

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:45.333
then you still want to get
outside and view sunlight.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:49.260
Some of you will wake up
before the sun comes out.

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:51.540
And if you're asking whether or not

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:52.710
turning on artificial lights

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:54.780
can replace sunlight at those hours,

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.870
unfortunately, the answer is no.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:21:59.217
Unless you have a very special light,

00:21:59.217 --> 00:22:01.290
and we'll talk about what kind of light,

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:04.320
the bright artificial lights
in your home environment

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:08.190
are not, I repeat, are not
going to be sufficiently bright

00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:11.190
to turn on the cortisol mechanism

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:12.720
and the other wake-up mechanisms

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:15.030
that you need early in the day.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.650
The diabolical twist, however,

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.410
is that those lights in
your home or apartment

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:21.030
or even on your phone

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.580
are bright enough to disrupt your sleep

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:25.310
if you look at them too late at night

00:22:25.310 --> 00:22:26.700
or in the middle of the night.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:30.900
So there's this asymmetry in
our retinal, our eye biology,

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:32.580
and in our brain's biology,

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:34.590
whereby early in the
day, right around waking,

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:37.050
you need a lot of light, a lot of photons,

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:38.190
a lot of light energy,

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:41.400
and artificial lights
generally just won't accomplish

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.930
what you need them to accomplish.

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.990
But at night, even a little
bit of artificial light

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:48.450
can really mess up your
so-called circadian,

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.830
your 24-hour clocks,

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:51.900
and all these mechanisms
that we're talking about.

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:55.020
So if you wake up before the
sun is out and it's still dark,

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.660
please turn on as many
bright artificial lights

00:22:57.660 --> 00:22:59.760
as you possibly can or need,

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:02.073
but then get outside once the sun is out.

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:06.450
On cloudy days, you especially
need to get outside.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.700
I repeat, on cloudy days, overcast days,

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:10.770
you especially need to get
outside and get sunlight.

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:12.210
You just need to get more of it.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:15.480
Now, how much light and how
much light viewing do you need?

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:18.690
This is going to vary
depending on person and place,

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.340
literally where you live on earth,

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.410
whether or not there's
a lot of tree cover,

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:24.210
whether or not you're somebody
who has sensitive eyes

00:23:24.210 --> 00:23:25.470
or less sensitive eyes.

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:26.700
It's really impossible for me

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.530
to give an absolute prescriptive,

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:30.240
but we can give some general guidelines.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.220
In general, on a clear day,

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:35.070
meaning no cloud cover
or minimal cloud cover,

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:39.000
you want to get this sunlight
exposure to your eyes

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.640
for about five minutes or so.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:42.720
Could be three minutes one day,

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.610
could be seven minutes the next day,

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:46.290
about five minutes.

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:48.600
On a day where there's cloud cover,

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:50.400
so the sun is just
peeking through the clouds

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.680
or it's more dense cloud cover,

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:56.220
you want to get about 10
minutes of sunlight exposure

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:57.660
to your eyes early in the day.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.150
And on days that are
really densely overcast

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:01.440
or maybe even are rainy,

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:04.620
you're going to want to get
as much as 20 or 30 minutes

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:06.660
of sunlight exposure.

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:10.260
Another key thing is do not, forget about,

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:13.020
just don't try and get
this sunlight exposure

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:15.990
through a windshield of a car or a window,

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:17.400
whether or not it's tinted or otherwise.

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:18.990
It takes far too long.

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:22.080
It's simply not going to
trigger the relevant mechanisms.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.400
You would be standing there all day

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:25.140
trying to get enough light into your eyes

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:26.070
from the morning sunlight

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:28.260
and by then the sun
will have already moved

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.270
from low solar angle to overhead

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:32.400
and it simply won't work
for all sorts of mechanisms

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.530
related to your circadian
rhythm functions.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.580
So just don't try and do it

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:40.050
through a windshield,
sunglasses, or a window.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:40.980
It's just not going to work.

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:42.000
Get outside.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.380
If the weather is really bad

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:44.910
or for whatever reason, safety reasons,

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.020
you cannot get outside,

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:47.850
well, then I suppose try
and get near a window.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:49.890
That would be the last, last resort.

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:51.840
But you really want to get outside

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:53.730
to get this sunlight exposure.

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:55.830
Now, if you live in a part of the world

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:57.930
where it's extremely dark and overcast

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:59.400
or the weather won't let you outside

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.670
or you live in a cave
or some other small box

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:05.070
that does not allow any
natural light into it

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:06.390
for whatever reason,

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.030
well, then you're going
to need a replacement

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.680
for that sunlight.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:14.040
And there are sunlight
simulators or daylight simulators

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:15.090
that you can purchase.

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:17.940
Those are quite expensive in general

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:21.060
and therefore I suggest cheaper options

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.560
that work just as well

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:24.060
because they get just as bright.

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:26.580
Things like ring lights that are sold

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:30.390
in order for people to take
selfies and this kind of thing.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:33.300
A drawing LED tablet
will work pretty well.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:34.133
I actually have one of those

00:25:34.133 --> 00:25:35.550
and I put it on my desk all morning

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:38.610
even though I still get
outside and look at sunlight

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:39.570
first thing in the morning,

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:43.590
again, also, especially, I
should say, on cloudy days.

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:45.240
We do not have any affiliation

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:49.020
to any ring lights or LED
lights or these panels.

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:51.690
So we will provide a link to
a couple of different options

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:53.220
if you want to explore
the various options.

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:54.750
I don't know what people's
different budgets are.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:56.400
I don't know where people live.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.110
I just know that many
of our listeners live

00:25:58.110 --> 00:25:59.250
in locations throughout the world

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:00.450
where, for instance, during the winter,

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:01.470
it gets very, very dark,

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.270
so they can't get sufficient sunlight.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:07.230
But get that morning light,
ideally from sunlight,

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:10.050
and take into account
all the specific points

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:11.430
that I've given you here.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:14.130
And, I should say, enjoy this practice.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.560
It's really nice to get outside
first thing in the morning

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:17.393
and get this sunlight.

00:26:17.393 --> 00:26:19.710
In fact, when you start doing this,

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:21.570
you'll notice that your body

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:24.060
will start to feel more energized

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.550
and it will feel more
energized more quickly.

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:27.690
You'll actually start

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:30.060
to notice this mechanism
kicking in each day,

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:32.309
especially if you're paying
attention to your physiology.

00:26:32.309 --> 00:26:34.950
So enjoy this practice of getting outside.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:37.650
Yes, you can take your
morning beverage outside.

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.570
Yes, you can take your dog with you.

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.580
In fact, animals intuitively know

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:42.750
to get this morning sunlight.

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:44.850
They actually seek it out
at the right times of days.

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:46.530
We human beings need to be told

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:47.940
by podcasters and other people

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:50.610
about the science that supports
these kinds of practices.

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:52.140
Our pets apparently do not.

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.930
But get outside alone or with somebody,

00:26:54.930 --> 00:26:56.550
with your kids, with your dog.

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:58.080
However you go about this practice,

00:26:58.080 --> 00:26:59.970
make sure you do this practice

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:03.120
at least 80% of the days of your life.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:03.960
That's right.

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.340
If you miss a day,

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:08.070
for instance, you're bedridden for a day,

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.774
try and get next to a window.

00:27:09.774 --> 00:27:11.940
Let's say you are traveling,

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.440
or for whatever reason,

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:16.140
you are not able to get outside
first thing in the morning,

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:18.810
well, then try to get twice
as much sunlight in your eyes,

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.460
or I should say extend the duration

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:22.050
of sunlight viewing in the morning

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:24.210
for twice as long the following day.

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:27.060
This is a slow, integrative mechanism

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:28.530
that underlies this whole thing

00:27:28.530 --> 00:27:29.363
of wakefulness during the day

00:27:29.363 --> 00:27:31.680
and sleep at night due to sunlight viewing

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:33.900
and if you miss a day, you can
make up for it the next day,

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:36.240
but you have to get twice as much light

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:39.450
or twice as much duration of light.

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:40.860
If you really want to get technical

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:41.820
and you really want to measure

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.200
how much light is in your environment,

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:46.530
you can download a free app,
something like Light Meter,

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:47.940
and that will allow your phone

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:49.650
to act as a bit of a light meter.

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:51.870
It'll give you a pretty
accurate measurement

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:54.600
of how many lux, which is
a measure of brightness,

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:56.520
are in your environment in the morning.

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:59.160
And in general, that's just
going to be a good tool

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:02.730
for evaluating your environments.

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:04.170
Here's what I suggest you do.

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:06.450
Wake up in the morning, take Light Meter,

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:08.310
point it at the brightest
light in your home,

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:09.180
and take a measurement,

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:11.400
and what you'll probably find
is it's about a thousand lux.

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:14.460
Now go outside and if
there's some sunlight out

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:15.480
and there's cloud cover,

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:17.640
point it at the sky and press that button.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:18.660
You can actually hold it down

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:22.200
and it'll give you a dynamically
updated lux measurement.

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:24.450
And what you'll find
is like 5,000, 10,000,

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:26.460
sometimes even 90,000 lux,

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.620
even though you don't experience
it as so much brighter,

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:30.540
and that's because an
indoor artificial light

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:33.810
is very concentrated
over a small spatial area

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.670
whereas the sunlight is very diffuse.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:37.680
But it's that diffuse,
very bright sunlight,

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:38.580
that photon energy,

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:39.413
that you really want

00:28:39.413 --> 00:28:41.490
that's going to set all the
rhythms of your brain and body

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.530
in the proper way.

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:44.880
Not just that cortisol peak,

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:47.160
but it's going to trigger
proper metabolism,

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:48.560
it's going to set a timer for you

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.270
to be able to fall asleep
about 16 hours later,

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:52.770
and on and on and on.

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:54.810
And I should mention within
the on and on and on,

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:57.660
it's also going to suppress any melatonin,

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:58.740
a hormone that makes you sleepy

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:00.930
that happens to be swimming
around in your bloodstream

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:02.460
at the time you wake up.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:03.930
It does a number of other things too,

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.030
including interact with
the adenosine system

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:07.890
and kind of wash out some of the adenosine

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:10.690
that might still be residual
if you didn't sleep enough.

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:14.901
Fundamentally speaking, get
that morning sunlight viewing.

00:29:14.901 --> 00:29:18.270
I promise you will be
grateful that you did.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:21.180
It makes everybody feel
better, feel more alert,

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:23.580
and it will greatly
assist with your ability

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.830
to fall and stay asleep later that night.

