00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:44.610
of anywhere from one to three minutes.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:46.740
This could be an ice bath
if that's your thing.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:48.210
It could be a cold tub,

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:50.550
or if you own a cold tub

00:31:50.550 --> 00:31:53.340
that's specifically designed
for deliberate cold exposure.

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.350
Get under some cold water.

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:57.600
That will certainly wake you up.

00:31:57.600 --> 00:31:59.490
And if you've ever jumped into cold water

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:00.360
or had a cold shower,

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:01.710
you know it really wakes you up

00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:04.950
because you release
adrenaline, epinephrine,

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:07.440
from both your brain and body,

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:08.307
the body from your adrenals

00:32:08.307 --> 00:32:10.110
and your brain from a
little cluster of neurons

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:11.370
called locus coeruleus.

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:12.870
Again, the names don't matter.

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:15.180
One to three minutes of cold
water exposure will wake you up

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:16.920
because of that adrenaline release

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:19.710
and, and I want to highlight the and,

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:23.490
it will serve to increase
your core body temperature.

00:32:23.490 --> 00:32:26.040
That's right, your body and brain interact

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:27.600
as a bit of a thermostat system

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.910
where if you put something cold
on the surface of your body,

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:31.710
your brain, a little cluster of neurons

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:33.900
in the so-called medial preoptic area,

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:35.587
act as a thermostat and say,

00:32:35.587 --> 00:32:38.640
"Ah, the external of my body is cold

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:40.350
and therefore I'm going to heat up

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:41.460
my core body temperature."

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:42.750
So it's a little bit paradoxical.

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:45.210
People think, oh, if you get
into cold water or an ice bath,

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:46.530
your body temperature is going to drop.

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:48.360
And indeed, that's true if
you stay in for a while,

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:50.340
but if you just get in for
about one to three minutes,

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:52.470
or under the cold shower
for one to three minutes,

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:55.140
your core body temperature will increase.

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:57.420
So then when you get
out of that cold water,

00:32:57.420 --> 00:33:01.020
your body temperature is
increasing at a rate, at a slope,

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:03.180
that's steeper than it would otherwise

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:04.380
and you're going to feel more alert.

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:05.340
It also has the advantage

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:07.080
of increasing not just adrenaline,

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:08.910
but dopamine, which is a molecule involved

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:10.380
in motivation, focus, et cetera.

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.180
So this is great for waking up.

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:13.290
So we've got sunlight,

00:33:13.290 --> 00:33:15.420
we've got temperature
triggered by cold water,

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:17.760
and we have exercise.

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:18.630
One of the best ways

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:21.720
to increase your core body
temperature early in the day

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:23.040
is to do exercise.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:24.900
Now, some of you might choose

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:27.810
to do your full-blown workout for the day

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:29.250
first thing when you
wake up in the morning,

00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:30.930
I would say the best time to exercise,

00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:32.700
at least what the research points to,

00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:35.130
is immediately when you
wake up in the morning

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:38.910
or three hours after waking
or 11 hours after waking.

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:41.580
But that's really getting
down into optimization

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:43.830
for sake of muscular
strength and grip strength

00:33:43.830 --> 00:33:47.370
and it's very hard to give
a strict prescriptive.

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:48.390
Here's what I suggest.

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:50.460
If you want to be alert early in the day

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:52.710
and you want to sleep great at night,

00:33:52.710 --> 00:33:55.530
get that bright sunlight,
get into some cold water,

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:57.810
and if you don't want to
get into some cold water,

00:33:57.810 --> 00:33:59.190
try and get some movement.

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:00.390
It could be a walk.

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:01.620
So you can get your sunlight exposure

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:04.260
while you're taking a walk
first thing in the morning.

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:05.400
It could be a light jog.

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:07.020
It could be skipping rope.

00:34:07.020 --> 00:34:09.540
These days, I skip rope for
about 10 minutes or 20 minutes

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:10.770
while looking at the sun.

00:34:10.770 --> 00:34:12.450
So I'm trying to layer
in these different things

00:34:12.450 --> 00:34:13.283
for waking up.

00:34:13.283 --> 00:34:14.940
And then I take a cold shower afterwards.

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:16.080
This is what I've been doing as of lately,

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:19.140
but I don't do that all
year long necessarily.

00:34:19.140 --> 00:34:22.260
Or some of you are going to
be working out mid-morning.

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:23.730
I sometimes do that.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:26.970
But try and get your core
body temperature increased

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:28.170
first thing in the morning,

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:31.140
and a great way to do that
is with the cold water

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:33.360
and/or with exercise.

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:35.730
And again, it doesn't have
to be your full-blown workout

00:34:35.730 --> 00:34:36.563
for the day

00:34:36.563 --> 00:34:37.920
if you're doing workouts consistently,

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:39.000
which I hope everybody is

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:41.160
because everybody really should exercise

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:43.500
at least, I believe, five or six,

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:44.580
or maybe even seven days a week.

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:46.560
For me, it's six days
a week, sometimes five,

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:48.120
rarely is it seven.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:49.500
So get that exercise

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:51.660
or even just a modest amount of movement,

00:34:51.660 --> 00:34:53.730
walking, jogging, skipping rope,

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:55.350
some light calisthenics.

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.300
That will further increase
your core body temperature

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:58.890
and help you feel more awake.

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:00.600
Then we have the category of caffeine.

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:01.470
And again, we're just talking

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:02.730
about this early part of the day,

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:04.050
and you might be saying, "Wait a second,

00:35:04.050 --> 00:35:06.390
I thought this was an episode
about tools for sleep."

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:07.950
Well, everything that
we're talking about doing

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:11.280
in these first 60 to 90 minutes of the day

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:15.630
really set in motion a
wave of biological cascades

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:17.460
that carry through the entire day

00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:18.780
and into the evening and into the night

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:20.820
and really do serve to optimize sleep.

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:22.590
So just hang in there with me.

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:23.670
And for those of you that are interested

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:25.860
in focus and attention,
your ability to learn,

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:27.450
all of these tools and practices

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:30.630
are going to greatly
enhance those as well.

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:32.340
So the next category of tool

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:34.380
for use early in the day is caffeine.

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:37.200
Caffeine is a very important
compound to think about.

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:39.900
I do realize that some people
who are prone to anxiety,

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:41.730
especially panic attacks, anxiety attacks,

00:35:41.730 --> 00:35:43.263
might avoid caffeine entirely.

00:35:44.130 --> 00:35:45.120
That's absolutely fine.

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:46.800
You do not have to drink caffeine.

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:48.270
So what I'm about to describe

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:50.610
are ways to leverage caffeine use

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:52.920
to optimize sleep and wakefulness

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:56.280
if you are comfortable with
caffeine, if you like caffeine,

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:57.360
I happen to love caffeine.

00:35:57.360 --> 00:36:00.030
I like it in the form
of coffee or espresso

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:01.530
or yerba mate tea,

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:04.860
in particular non-smoked
varieties of yerba mate tea.

00:36:04.860 --> 00:36:07.440
Non-smoked because the smoked
varieties seem to carry

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:09.690
some carcinogenics, some
cancer causing risk.

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:10.980
There's increasing data on that.

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:14.640
So non-smoked varieties of yerba mate.

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:15.960
So caffeine is something

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:18.510
that a lot of people
consume early in the day.

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:20.790
How much depends on your tolerance,

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:22.890
and there's a lot of
individual variability here.

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:25.350
Again, caffeine is an
adenosine antagonist,

00:36:25.350 --> 00:36:27.720
or effectively works as
an adenosine antagonist

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:28.890
and limits sleepiness.

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:30.210
I highly recommend

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.130
that everybody delay their caffeine intake

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:34.770
for 90 to 120 minutes after waking.

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:37.050
However painful it may
be to eventually arrive

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:39.870
at that 90 to 120 minutes after waking,

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:42.270
you want, and I encourage you,

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:44.580
to clear out whatever residual adenosine

00:36:44.580 --> 00:36:46.050
is circulating in your system

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:48.270
in that first 90 to
120 minutes of the day.

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:49.290
Get that sunlight exposure,

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:50.880
get some movement to wake up,

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:55.200
and then, and only then,
start to ingest caffeine

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:58.410
because what you'll do if
you delay caffeine intake

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:01.020
until 90 to 120 minutes after waking

00:37:01.020 --> 00:37:04.530
is you will avoid the
so-called afternoon crash.

00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:06.840
And you may still get a
little bit of dip in energy

00:37:06.840 --> 00:37:07.673
in the afternoon,

00:37:07.673 --> 00:37:09.540
but it's not going to
be that massive crash.

00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:11.940
I've talked about the
reasons for that crash

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:13.230
on previous episodes.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:15.330
But if you delay your caffeine intake

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:17.040
90 to 120 minutes after waking,

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:20.040
you are doing yourself a great
service towards wakefulness

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:21.390
and to avoid the crash.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:23.490
And the afternoon crash has
another liability to it,

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:24.810
which is typically people will emerge

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:27.540
from that afternoon crash
either grumpy or groggy

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:30.420
and then they'll lean into
drinking more caffeine,

00:37:30.420 --> 00:37:32.760
which can then disrupt their sleep.

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:35.580
So wait 90 to 120 minutes
after waking in the morning

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:36.810
to drink caffeine.

00:37:36.810 --> 00:37:40.808
And if you drink caffeine at
any point throughout the day,

00:37:40.808 --> 00:37:43.230
really try and avoid any caffeine,

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:44.940
certainly avoid drinking

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:48.630
more than a hundred milligrams
of caffeine after 4:00 p.m.

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:51.450
and probably even better to
limit your last caffeine intake

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:53.610
to 3:00 p.m. or even 2:00 p.m.

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:56.400
And for many people,
shifting that caffeine intake

00:37:56.400 --> 00:37:58.560
from immediately after
waking in the morning

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.060
to 90 to 120 minutes

00:38:00.060 --> 00:38:02.850
gives them a much longer arc
of energy throughout the day

00:38:02.850 --> 00:38:04.860
and they don't feel the need

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:07.590
to drink more caffeine
later in the afternoon.

00:38:07.590 --> 00:38:09.840
If you do drink caffeine
later in the afternoon,

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:12.750
really try and limit the
total amount or drink decaf.

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:14.910
Certainly keep the total amount

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:16.260
to less than a hundred milligrams

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:17.100
if you are interested

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:18.810
in getting into the best possible sleep.

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:21.750
And I say this knowing that
many people, including myself,

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:23.490
can drink a double espresso

00:38:23.490 --> 00:38:25.410
with 200 milligrams of caffeine or more

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:28.410
at 5:00 p.m. or even
6:00 p.m. or after dinner

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:30.780
and still, quote,
unquote, fall asleep fine

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:31.890
or still sleep fine.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:34.530
However, there are terrific data,

00:38:34.530 --> 00:38:36.660
Matt Walker and I talked about this,

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:38.880
and there are more and
more papers all the time

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:42.930
that point to the fact that
caffeine intake late in the day,

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:44.310
after 4:00 p.m. that is,

00:38:44.310 --> 00:38:46.650
can really disrupt the
architecture of your sleep.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:48.060
So you might think you're sleeping well,

00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:49.920
but you're not sleeping
nearly as well as you could

00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:53.040
if you avoided caffeine
in those afternoon hours.

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:56.010
Now, some of you might be doing
your main about of exercise

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:57.000
first thing in the morning

00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:59.730
and you want your caffeine
before that about of exercise.

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:02.190
In that case, I say, go for it.

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:03.023
Drink your caffeine,

00:39:03.023 --> 00:39:04.800
do your workout right after waking up.

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:06.660
I don't have a problem with that.

00:39:06.660 --> 00:39:07.650
You will find, however,

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:10.167
that you're going to get an
early afternoon dip in energy

00:39:10.167 --> 00:39:12.840
and that dip in energy is
going to be substantial

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:14.910
because it's going to be a dip in energy

00:39:14.910 --> 00:39:18.870
that naturally follows that
workout from the morning.

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:21.270
So it's dependent on temperature,

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:23.250
and it's going to be related

00:39:23.250 --> 00:39:27.780
to the elimination of that
adenosine blockade by caffeine.

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:30.900
So you're getting a
kind of a one-two punch

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:31.890
on your energy levels

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:36.600
by taking a lot of caffeine and
exercising early in the day.

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:37.710
You can sort of expect

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:39.840
that you're going to get a drop in energy

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:41.160
in the early afternoon.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:42.900
That's okay if that works for you,

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:45.090
but just know that delaying that caffeine

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:47.550
90 to 120 minutes after waking

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:51.753
would be the ideal scenario
most days and most scenarios.

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:55.680
All that said, I
absolutely respect the fact

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:57.990
that people have different work schedules,

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:00.000
kid schedules, et cetera.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.760
So if you want to do some
or none or all these tools,

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:03.593
that's really up to you.

00:40:03.593 --> 00:40:05.040
I'm just providing them to you

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:08.610
in the simplest form that I
can possibly provide them.

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:10.350
Now, the other lever or tool

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:12.570
that you have available to you is food.

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:14.640
Not just what you eat, but when you eat.

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:17.700
And it turns out that if
you eat early in the day,

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:21.120
you support a biological clock mechanism

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:24.240
that will make you more
alert early in the day.

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:26.160
That said, many people choose to fast

00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:27.870
in the early morning hours of the day

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:29.040
or in the first part of the day.

00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:29.910
I'm one such person.

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.640
I generally don't ingest any food

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:35.190
until about 11:00 a.m. or 12 noon.

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:36.750
Sometimes I'll have a protein shake.

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:38.100
Sometimes I'll have some almonds.

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:39.240
Sometimes I'll have breakfast.

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:40.860
If people are meeting
for brunch or breakfast,

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:42.886
I will have breakfast for social reasons

00:40:42.886 --> 00:40:43.890
every once in a while.

00:40:43.890 --> 00:40:47.206
But most of the time I don't
eat until about lunch time.

00:40:47.206 --> 00:40:49.050
However, some people are really hungry

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:50.160
when they wake up in the morning.

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:53.160
Just know that if you
eat early in the day,

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:56.640
you are further triggering
an increase in metabolism

00:40:56.640 --> 00:40:59.970
and in temperature that
will make you more alert.

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:02.130
So you don't have to eat early in the day,

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:03.300
but you can start to see

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:04.980
how these different tools layer together.

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:08.430
Sunlight viewing, exercise,
cold water, eating.

00:41:08.430 --> 00:41:10.860
Many of them are converging
on the same mechanisms.

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:11.940
In fact, when you drink caffeine,

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:14.130
there's also a small
increase in body temperature

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:16.217
due to the adrenaline
increase that it stimulates.

00:41:16.217 --> 00:41:19.500
So all of these things can be
layered on top of one another

00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:20.970
or you can use them individually

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:22.350
or think about them individually.

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:25.230
Now, food is an interesting lever or tool

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:26.760
because it's not just about when you eat,

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:28.740
but it's also about what you eat.

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:31.620
And I've talked a lot
about eating for energy

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:33.600
and what that means in
terms of caloric energy

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:35.490
versus neural energy, et cetera,

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:37.080
in previous podcast episodes.

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:38.370
We're not going to focus on that now

00:41:38.370 --> 00:41:40.380
because, frankly, to
get into a description

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:42.450
of whether or not somebody
should eat fruits or vegetables

00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:45.270
or animal proteins or dairy,
et cetera, early in the day,

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:46.350
that's very nuanced.

00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:47.640
What you eat for your breakfast,

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:48.930
or if you choose to not eat breakfast,

00:41:48.930 --> 00:41:50.310
is really up to you.

00:41:50.310 --> 00:41:53.550
All that said, if you
eat a very large meal,

00:41:53.550 --> 00:41:56.790
it doesn't matter if you
slept terrifically well

00:41:56.790 --> 00:41:58.260
10 hours the night before

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:00.180
or if you are about to go to sleep

00:42:00.180 --> 00:42:02.010
or if it's the middle of the afternoon,

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:04.110
if your gut is full of food,

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:06.150
there's just a large
volume of food in your gut,

00:42:06.150 --> 00:42:09.030
it's going to divert a lot of blood

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:11.430
and other critical resources

00:42:11.430 --> 00:42:13.110
away from other organs of your body,

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:14.550
in particular, your brain,

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:16.890
and you're going to be sleepy
after eating a big meal.

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:18.240
So this is sort of a duh,

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:20.730
but I think oftentimes in the discussions

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:22.440
about what to eat for energy,

00:42:22.440 --> 00:42:25.050
people neglect to consider food volume

00:42:25.050 --> 00:42:28.350
as a strong parameter or
variable in that discussion.

00:42:28.350 --> 00:42:29.700
So if you eat a huge breakfast,

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:31.830
it's likely that you are going to be tired

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:33.480
immediately after eating that breakfast

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:35.700
unless of course you exercise
very hard prior to that

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.640
and you metabolize all
that food very quickly.

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:40.470
So it's up to you whether or not to eat

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:42.300
first thing in the morning or not.

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:45.360
But if you do eat in the first
few hours of the morning,

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:47.250
just understand that you are setting

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:48.870
or you are helping to set

00:42:48.870 --> 00:42:52.620
a food entrained, as it's
called, circadian clock.

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:56.300
Light, temperature, timing of food intake,

00:42:56.300 --> 00:42:58.140
movement and exercise,

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:02.430
all of these things literally
funnel in in a neural sense,

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:05.550
they funnel into this thing
that we call the circadian clock

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:08.430
and they let that clock,
that set of neurons,

00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:11.670
predict when you are likely
to be eating and active

00:43:11.670 --> 00:43:13.680
and viewing sunlight

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:15.450
the next day and the next
day and the next day.

00:43:15.450 --> 00:43:18.120
I say all this because there
are some beautiful studies,

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:20.460
and I'll highlight one, again,
in the show note captions,

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:22.770
that show that if people
are having a hard time

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:24.180
waking up in the morning,

00:43:24.180 --> 00:43:25.590
one of the things they can do

00:43:25.590 --> 00:43:29.280
is maximize sunlight viewing,
exercise in the morning,

00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:30.150
drink caffeine.

00:43:30.150 --> 00:43:32.730
Although, again, I support the idea

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:33.960
that that would best be done

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:36.543
about 90 to 120 minutes after waking.

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:40.500
Eating some food in those
early morning hours,

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:41.333
et cetera, et cetera.

00:43:41.333 --> 00:43:45.030
You can layer in multiple levers or tools

00:43:45.030 --> 00:43:46.680
in order to be more alert.

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:50.580
And that's what these levers
and tools are really there for

00:43:50.580 --> 00:43:52.680
in this sense of what
we're talking about today,

00:43:52.680 --> 00:43:54.300
which is optimizing sleep.

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:55.650
Yes, they will make you more alert.

00:43:55.650 --> 00:43:57.990
Yes, they will provide some
adrenaline and dopamine,

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:00.390
for instance, the cold
water, et cetera, et cetera.

00:44:00.390 --> 00:44:02.700
But the reason we're
talking about these things

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:04.410
in the context of sleep

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:06.630
is that they start to give your body

00:44:06.630 --> 00:44:09.630
some predictable autonomic timing.

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:11.640
What is predictable autonomic timing?

00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:12.870
Well, your autonomic nervous system

00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:15.210
is the components of your brain and body

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:18.180
that cause wakefulness and sleepiness

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:21.180
and you can start to
create some predictability

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:22.620
in that autonomic timing.

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:24.990
You can start to do things
that really make it such

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:28.200
that you naturally wake
up at six in the morning

00:44:28.200 --> 00:44:29.130
or five in the morning.

00:44:29.130 --> 00:44:30.120
That's right, if you're somebody

00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:32.220
who naturally is a night owl,

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:34.950
who likes to stay up
until two in the morning

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:36.480
and sleep until 10:00 a.m.,

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:38.610
and you now have a job or
you have to go to school

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:40.170
or you have a partner
that likes to get up early

00:44:40.170 --> 00:44:41.700
and go to sleep early,

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:43.080
well, you can make that happen

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:45.300
and you can make that
happen pretty painlessly

00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:47.040
if you take a week or so

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:50.910
and go to sleep 30 minutes or
an hour earlier each night,

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:51.743
set an alarm

00:44:51.743 --> 00:44:56.070
and wake up 30 minutes or
an hour earlier each morning

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:57.150
until, of course, you're waking up

00:44:57.150 --> 00:44:58.140
at the time you want to wake up,

00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:00.333
and then even in that groggy state,

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:03.330
get some exercise, get
some sunlight viewing.

00:45:03.330 --> 00:45:04.200
If the sun's not out,

00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:06.120
turn on those bright artificial lights.

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:08.220
Have some breakfast, even
if you're not hungry.

00:45:08.220 --> 00:45:11.160
In fact, for those of you
that engage in shift work

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:12.000
because you have to,

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:14.010
or travel and you're jet lagged,

00:45:14.010 --> 00:45:17.190
one of the quickest ways to
shift your circadian clock

00:45:17.190 --> 00:45:18.510
and get onto the local schedule

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:20.640
is to eat on the local schedule.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:24.690
So what all these tools do is
they really set up a cascade.

00:45:24.690 --> 00:45:27.360
Think of it as kind of a
wavefront of wakefulness and focus

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.320
throughout the day.

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:29.427
It'll take you through
the middle of the day

00:45:29.427 --> 00:45:32.460
and the afternoon stage we'll
talk about in a few minutes,

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:35.160
but really they take you to this period

00:45:35.160 --> 00:45:38.670
that is about 5:00 p.m.
until your bedtime.

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:40.500
I realize some people are
going to bed very early,

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:43.470
like 8:00 p.m. or 9:00 p.m.,
which to me seems very early,

00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:45.870
but very few people go to
sleep at 5:00 p.m. right?

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:48.480
Unless you're doing that for
shift work or other reasons.

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:52.650
But from 5:00 p.m. until bedtime
is really a critical period

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:55.020
in which you need to
leverage particular tools

00:45:55.020 --> 00:45:58.260
in order to get and stay asleep optimally

00:45:58.260 --> 00:45:59.850
and to be able to sleep through the night.

00:45:59.850 --> 00:46:01.710
So really there are three critical periods

00:46:01.710 --> 00:46:03.480
throughout each 24-hour cycle.

00:46:03.480 --> 00:46:05.220
And during each of those critical periods,

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:06.720
you're going to want to do

00:46:06.720 --> 00:46:09.150
as many specific things as you can

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:10.710
to optimize your wakefulness

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:12.480
and focus and mood throughout the day

00:46:12.480 --> 00:46:15.090
and your sleep at night.

00:46:15.090 --> 00:46:15.990
The first critical period

00:46:15.990 --> 00:46:17.880
is the one that we've been
talking about up until now.

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:19.230
Things like morning sunlight viewing,

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:21.480
caffeine 90 to 120 minutes after waking,

00:46:21.480 --> 00:46:22.920
exercise, and so on.

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:24.570
We can call that critical period one

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:27.630
and it really encompasses the
time from which you wake up

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:30.150
until about three hours after waking.

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:31.440
Although, I should just mention

00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:32.347
'cause there are always
those people that say,

00:46:32.347 --> 00:46:33.750
"Wait, I wake up at 4:00 a.m.

00:46:33.750 --> 00:46:35.330
and the sun isn't out until 8:00 a.m.,"

00:46:35.330 --> 00:46:37.170
okay, so it might be four hours.

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:41.100
But really it's those early
morning hours of your day

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:42.450
once you're awake.

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:44.160
The second critical period

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:47.070
is the time throughout
the day and afternoon

00:46:47.070 --> 00:46:48.360
leading into evening.

00:46:48.360 --> 00:46:49.980
So you may ask what are
the things that you can do

00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:50.970
throughout the day,

00:46:50.970 --> 00:46:51.810
the middle of your day

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:53.520
and into the afternoon and evening hours,

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:54.630
that are really going to set you up

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:57.210
for the best possible
sleep later that night.

00:46:57.210 --> 00:47:00.330
Well, there are a few dos
and there are a few don'ts.

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:01.320
First of all,

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:03.690
be careful about ingesting
too much caffeine

00:47:03.690 --> 00:47:04.680
throughout the middle of the day.

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:05.760
That's kind of an obvious one

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:08.040
for the reasons that we
talked about earlier.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:10.950
Second of all, if you are a napper,

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:12.150
and I raise my hand now,

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:12.983
for those of you listening,

00:47:12.983 --> 00:47:15.180
I'm raising my right
hand because I love naps.

00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:16.470
I've always loved naps.

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:20.730
Nowadays I do NSDR or
a Reveri sleep hypnosis

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:22.260
almost every day.

00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:23.910
And I tend to do that, as I mentioned,

00:47:23.910 --> 00:47:25.410
in the early afternoon hours

00:47:25.410 --> 00:47:26.910
if I'm feeling kind of sleepy,

00:47:26.910 --> 00:47:28.080
because even though I optimize

00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:30.360
my caffeine intake timing, et cetera,

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:32.310
I tend to get a little
sleepy in the afternoon.

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:34.140
Most people get a little
sleepy in the afternoon.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:35.100
Some of that is related

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:37.080
to hitting that peak of body temperature.

00:47:37.080 --> 00:47:39.660
And you might think, wait, I
thought high body temperature

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:41.400
is associated with alertness,

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:42.233
and it is,

00:47:42.233 --> 00:47:44.490
but right as you crest
that high body temperature

00:47:44.490 --> 00:47:46.230
and your body temperature starts to drop,

00:47:46.230 --> 00:47:47.970
there's a tendency to
be a little bit sleepy.

00:47:47.970 --> 00:47:51.120
So some of you might opt to
take a nap in the afternoon.

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:52.920
Should you nap, should you not nap?

00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:54.480
That's a question that I get asked a lot

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:56.070
and that I asked Dr. Matthew Walker

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:57.630
when he was a guest on this podcast.

00:47:57.630 --> 00:48:00.480
Here was his answer and
here's what the data support.

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:03.120
It is fine to nap in the afternoon,

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:06.570
but don't nap so late in
the day or for so long

00:48:06.570 --> 00:48:07.980
that it disrupts your ability

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:10.110
to fall and stay asleep at night

00:48:10.110 --> 00:48:11.940
for your major sleep about, okay?

00:48:11.940 --> 00:48:13.260
So naps are fine,

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:15.360
but don't sleep so long during the day

00:48:15.360 --> 00:48:16.920
or too late in the day

00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:20.040
that it disrupts your ability
to fall and stay asleep.

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:23.043
I should also say you do not have to nap.

00:48:23.043 --> 00:48:24.600
It's kind of an interesting phenomenon

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:26.910
that happens on these
podcasts and on social media

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:28.020
where we'll talk about naps

00:48:28.020 --> 00:48:29.940
and the fact that naps are great,

00:48:29.940 --> 00:48:31.950
but don't make them
longer than 90 minutes,

00:48:31.950 --> 00:48:34.290
but then all the non-nappers
get really worried.

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:35.490
Like, wait, am I supposed to nap?

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:37.170
I don't like naps. I wake up groggy.

00:48:37.170 --> 00:48:38.490
You do not have to nap.

00:48:38.490 --> 00:48:39.330
In fact, if you can make it

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:40.980
through your whole day without napping,

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:42.150
great, more power to you.

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:45.750
But if you do nap and you
find that naps serve you well,

00:48:45.750 --> 00:48:47.550
keep those naps shorter than 90 minutes

00:48:47.550 --> 00:48:50.400
for reasons related to
ultradian cycles and so forth,

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:53.460
and make sure that you don't
nap too late in the day

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:55.260
that you are then staying
up too late at night

00:48:55.260 --> 00:48:57.660
and having a hard time
waking up the next morning.

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:00.780
I will say that for a lot of
people who do not like naps

00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:04.050
or that find they wake up
really grumpy from naps

00:49:04.050 --> 00:49:06.000
or groggy from naps,

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:09.660
I encourage you to try the Reveri app,

00:49:09.660 --> 00:49:12.930
try an NSDR script, try yoga nidra.

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:14.130
Try something of that sort

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:16.980
for anywhere from 10 to 20 to 30 minutes.

00:49:16.980 --> 00:49:19.350
I tend to do this every day now.

00:49:19.350 --> 00:49:21.480
I'll just lie down, and I love yoga nidra,

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:22.740
I love NSDR scripts,

00:49:22.740 --> 00:49:24.330
I love using the Reveri app.

00:49:24.330 --> 00:49:27.000
In particular, the
portion of the Reveri app

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:28.590
that gets you better at sleeping.

00:49:28.590 --> 00:49:30.900
It really is beneficial for me

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:33.660
because it serves as very replenishing

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:35.160
while I'm doing that hypnosis,

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:36.570
but it's also gotten me much better

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:37.890
at falling and staying asleep

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:40.080
and falling back asleep in
the middle of the night.

00:49:40.080 --> 00:49:42.210
So this critical period throughout the day

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:44.070
is one in which most people
are doing a lot of stuff.

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:45.630
They're emailing and picking up kids

00:49:45.630 --> 00:49:47.910
and they're exercising
and they're commuting

00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:48.750
and doing all sorts of things,

00:49:48.750 --> 00:49:50.700
taking phone calls and Zooms, et cetera.

00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:53.340
But if you can get that
period of deep relaxation

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:55.410
through a nap or NSDR,

00:49:55.410 --> 00:49:56.670
that's going to serve you well.

00:49:56.670 --> 00:49:58.230
Try not to drink too much caffeine,

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:01.320
certainly no more than a
hundred milligrams of caffeine,

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:02.310
after 4:00 p.m

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:05.310
if your goal is to fall asleep
at a reasonably normal time.

00:50:05.310 --> 00:50:08.820
And for those of you that
exercise in the afternoon,

00:50:08.820 --> 00:50:12.180
understand that if you
exercise very intensely,

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:13.740
so this might be weight
training or running

00:50:13.740 --> 00:50:15.870
or some other very intense exercise,

00:50:15.870 --> 00:50:17.940
typically that's going to further increase

00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:19.350
your body temperature.

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:20.183
Makes sense, right?

00:50:20.183 --> 00:50:21.016
Based on everything we know

00:50:21.016 --> 00:50:22.527
about metabolism and body temperature.

00:50:22.527 --> 00:50:26.220
And it's going to so-called
delay your circadian clock.

00:50:26.220 --> 00:50:27.360
It's going to make it such

00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:30.630
that you want to fall
asleep a little bit later,

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:31.980
maybe even a lot later.

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:34.740
So if you're exercising in
the afternoon or evening

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:36.180
and that's the only time you can exercise

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:38.220
or that's the time that
you prefer to exercise,

00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:41.460
great, but be careful about
ingesting too much caffeine

00:50:41.460 --> 00:50:43.350
in order to get the
energy to do that exercise

00:50:43.350 --> 00:50:45.720
'cause that caffeine
will disrupt your sleep

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:49.680
and just know that you are
delaying your circadian clock.

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:50.640
You are making it such

00:50:50.640 --> 00:50:53.130
that you will naturally
want to go to sleep later

00:50:53.130 --> 00:50:54.780
and wake up later.

00:50:54.780 --> 00:50:57.780
Contrast that with if you
exercise early in the day,

00:50:57.780 --> 00:50:59.580
say, immediately after waking up

00:50:59.580 --> 00:51:03.930
or in the first zero to
four hours after waking,

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:05.880
in most cases, that's not going to shift

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:07.560
your circadian clock much.

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:09.120
And toward the end of the episode,

00:51:09.120 --> 00:51:12.060
we'll talk a little bit
about forced exercise

00:51:12.060 --> 00:51:13.470
prior to wake-up times.

00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:14.970
That doesn't mean doing
exercise in your sleep.

00:51:14.970 --> 00:51:16.440
That means deliberately setting an alarm

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:17.700
and getting out of bed much earlier

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:18.900
than you naturally would.

00:51:18.900 --> 00:51:21.150
That turns out to be a very potent tool

00:51:21.150 --> 00:51:25.800
to so-called advance your circadian clock.

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:28.260
So we can talk about that a
little bit later in the episode.

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:31.500
But this critical period,
too, in the middle of the day

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:33.960
is when you're going to want
to leverage specific tools,

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:35.820
and we've talked about those:

00:51:35.820 --> 00:51:37.470
limiting caffeine intake;

00:51:37.470 --> 00:51:40.950
being mindful of the
clock-delaying effects of exercise;

00:51:40.950 --> 00:51:43.410
the fact that, also,
if you're going to nap,

00:51:43.410 --> 00:51:47.610
you don't want to nap too
long or too late into the day

00:51:47.610 --> 00:51:49.590
otherwise you'll disrupt
your nighttime sleep.

00:51:49.590 --> 00:51:52.350
So this critical period two,

00:51:52.350 --> 00:51:53.820
or second critical period, I should say,

00:51:53.820 --> 00:51:54.960
during the middle of the day

00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:56.910
is a time in which you should
be doing certain things

00:51:56.910 --> 00:51:58.380
and avoiding doing certain things.

00:51:58.380 --> 00:51:59.730
So that raises the
question of whether or not

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:01.770
you should also be getting a lot of light,

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:03.720
in particular, sunlight,
throughout the day.

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:05.340
Now, that's something that
hasn't been explored too much

00:52:05.340 --> 00:52:06.173
in the literature

00:52:06.173 --> 00:52:08.340
until recently when Dr. Samer Hattar

00:52:08.340 --> 00:52:10.710
who's the director of
the chronobiology unit

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:12.420
at the National Institutes
of Mental Health,

00:52:12.420 --> 00:52:13.680
decided to do a number of experiments

00:52:13.680 --> 00:52:15.750
exploring the effects of light on mood

00:52:15.750 --> 00:52:18.570
and other aspects of brain
function and body function

00:52:18.570 --> 00:52:20.340
when that light is delivered
not just in the morning,

00:52:20.340 --> 00:52:21.390
which is great for us,

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:22.860
but also throughout the day.

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:24.900
So should you be looking at sunlight

00:52:24.900 --> 00:52:27.219
or bright artificial
lights throughout the day?

00:52:27.219 --> 00:52:28.260
Now, on the face of it,

00:52:28.260 --> 00:52:30.480
you might just think, yes,
you know, sunlight's great.

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:32.280
Provided we're not getting a sunburn

00:52:32.280 --> 00:52:34.770
and we're not staring at the
sun and damaging our eyes,

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:37.110
we should get as much
sunlight as we possibly can.

00:52:37.110 --> 00:52:39.630
In fact, we talked about this
in the episode on hormones

00:52:39.630 --> 00:52:42.394
about how getting light
onto as much of our skin

00:52:42.394 --> 00:52:44.190
as we can throughout the day

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:45.510
can really help in the production

00:52:45.510 --> 00:52:47.550
of testosterone and estrogen
in both men and women

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:49.770
in healthy ways that
improves mood and libido

00:52:49.770 --> 00:52:53.760
and all sorts of things that
are associated with wellbeing.

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:56.310
However, because light is
such a powerful stimulus

00:52:56.310 --> 00:52:57.570
for controlling the timing

00:52:57.570 --> 00:53:00.420
of your sleepfulness, or
sleepiness, I should say,

00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:02.370
and wakefulness,

00:53:02.370 --> 00:53:03.510
we might want to be cautious

00:53:03.510 --> 00:53:06.150
about how much light we are
viewing in the afternoon,

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:09.090
in particular, in the
early evening hours, right?

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:12.000
Well, turns out it's
not so straightforward.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:14.940
Viewing, so sunlight to the eyes, sunlight

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:17.220
in the late afternoon and evening hours,

00:53:17.220 --> 00:53:18.810
so again, depends on time of year,

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:21.120
depends on location that
you happen to be in,

00:53:21.120 --> 00:53:22.530
but getting some sunlight in your eyes

00:53:22.530 --> 00:53:26.520
for, again, maybe five
or 10, maybe 30 minutes,

00:53:26.520 --> 00:53:28.470
depending on how much
cloud cover there is,

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:30.330
doing that in the afternoon

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:33.210
serves an additional beneficial purpose,

00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:37.860
which is you protect or you
inoculate your nervous system

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:39.930
against some of the negative effects

00:53:39.930 --> 00:53:41.550
of bright artificial light

00:53:41.550 --> 00:53:46.050
or even dim artificial
light in the nighttime hours

00:53:46.050 --> 00:53:47.370
between 10:00 p.m. and 4:00 a.m.,

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:49.320
which is really critical period three.

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:51.780
And we'll talk about what
to do and what to not do

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:54.810
during critical period three
of every 24-hour cycle.

00:53:54.810 --> 00:53:57.360
But to make it very clear
what I'm saying here,

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:59.400
get that morning sunlight in your eyes,

00:53:59.400 --> 00:54:01.470
but also get some sunlight in your eyes

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:03.690
in the late afternoon and evening hours

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:05.760
when the sun is at
so-called low solar angle,

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:07.890
when it starts to descend in the sky.

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:09.720
Again, you don't have to
stare directly at the sun,

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:11.580
although if you can catch
a nice, beautiful sunset,

00:54:11.580 --> 00:54:12.420
go for it.

00:54:12.420 --> 00:54:15.052
But as the sun starts to descend,

00:54:15.052 --> 00:54:18.889
it triggers those same neurons in your eye

00:54:18.889 --> 00:54:21.090
that communicate with
your circadian clock,

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:24.150
but it communicates with
a different component

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:26.340
or different compartment
within the circadian clock.

00:54:26.340 --> 00:54:28.560
That circadian clock
is not just one thing.

00:54:28.560 --> 00:54:29.393
It's multiple things.

00:54:29.393 --> 00:54:30.990
And you have what are
called morning oscillators

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:32.070
and evening oscillators.

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:33.810
And to make a long story short,

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:37.530
the tool that I'm describing
of looking at the sun

00:54:37.530 --> 00:54:39.510
in the late afternoon and evening,

00:54:39.510 --> 00:54:41.760
again, blinking is fine,

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:42.690
don't stare at the sun,

00:54:42.690 --> 00:54:43.980
but getting that sunlight in your eyes

00:54:43.980 --> 00:54:45.780
in the late afternoon and evening

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:50.130
signals to that clock
that it's evening time

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:51.960
and that sleep is coming.

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:56.460
It also serves as a second
anchor or reference point

00:54:56.460 --> 00:54:59.880
for your body and your brain
to know where it is in time.

00:54:59.880 --> 00:55:01.500
Remember back to the
beginning of the episode

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:03.840
when I said your brain and
your body and all your organs

00:55:03.840 --> 00:55:06.060
are locked inside this skin and this skull

00:55:06.060 --> 00:55:08.340
and they don't know what's
going on in the outside world.

00:55:08.340 --> 00:55:09.777
Well, that morning sunlight viewing

00:55:09.777 --> 00:55:12.870
and the other things you do
during critical period one,

00:55:12.870 --> 00:55:15.420
those provide one strong set of signals

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:16.827
that it's wake-up time
and time to be alert

00:55:16.827 --> 00:55:18.330
and time to be focused.

00:55:18.330 --> 00:55:20.190
And then in the evening,

00:55:20.190 --> 00:55:23.610
by getting sunlight in your eyes again,

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:25.260
in particular, sunlight that comes

00:55:25.260 --> 00:55:27.810
from low solar angle sunlight,

00:55:27.810 --> 00:55:29.850
well, that provides a second stimulus

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:31.440
or a second reference point

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:33.990
that tells your brain and
body, "Hey, it's evening.

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:35.700
The sun is descending."

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:36.533
Now, you might say,

00:55:36.533 --> 00:55:38.610
"Wait, how does the brain
and these neurons know

00:55:38.610 --> 00:55:40.560
the difference between morning
light and evening light?"

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:41.670
It turns out has to do

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:43.890
with the particular wavelengths of light

00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:46.170
that are present in
morning versus evening.

00:55:46.170 --> 00:55:47.820
It's an incredible mechanism.

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:50.760
And you are probably
familiar with the fact

00:55:50.760 --> 00:55:52.770
that when the sun is directly overhead,

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:55.500
it's really bright white and yellow

00:55:55.500 --> 00:55:56.760
and the sky's often blue,

00:55:56.760 --> 00:55:57.690
and if there's cloud cover,

00:55:57.690 --> 00:55:59.580
it just comes through as
a bunch of bright light,

00:55:59.580 --> 00:56:00.990
well, next time you're out in the morning,

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:02.670
take a look at what a sunrise looks like.

00:56:02.670 --> 00:56:04.860
There's a lot of yellow-blue contrast,

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:06.570
and those yellow blues signal

00:56:06.570 --> 00:56:09.630
important specific sets of
cells in your eye and brain

00:56:09.630 --> 00:56:10.620
that it's morning.

00:56:10.620 --> 00:56:13.110
In the evening, you're also
going to see yellow and blue,

00:56:13.110 --> 00:56:16.440
but the ratio of yellows
and blues has now changed

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:17.760
and you also see some oranges,

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:20.339
and in a really brilliant
sunset, you'll see some reds.

00:56:20.339 --> 00:56:22.320
If you haven't noticed this already,

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:23.430
you'll really want to look for this.

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:25.890
It's really kind of fun
and cool to look at.

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:28.830
Well, those yellows and blues and oranges

00:56:28.830 --> 00:56:30.330
that you see in the evening sunsets,

00:56:30.330 --> 00:56:33.330
those signal to your brain
and body that evening is there

00:56:33.330 --> 00:56:34.770
and that nighttime is coming

00:56:34.770 --> 00:56:36.120
and they're really establishing

00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:39.960
a second reference point or
wavefront of biological signals

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:42.237
that are going to optimize
your nighttime hours

00:56:42.237 --> 00:56:45.120
and your transition into
really terrific sleep.

00:56:45.120 --> 00:56:46.170
So now let's talk

00:56:46.170 --> 00:56:48.780
about what I'm calling
critical period three

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:50.160
of each 24-hour cycle.

00:56:50.160 --> 00:56:53.010
So this would be the period
of time of late evening,

00:56:53.010 --> 00:56:55.560
So it might be 6:00 p.m. for some,

00:56:55.560 --> 00:56:56.610
depending on when you go to sleep,

00:56:56.610 --> 00:56:57.600
or 7:00 p.m.

00:56:57.600 --> 00:56:59.970
extending into the hours

00:56:59.970 --> 00:57:02.280
in which you decide to get
into bed and go to sleep

00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:03.870
and then throughout the night.

00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:04.703
There are a number of things

00:57:04.703 --> 00:57:05.760
that you're going to want to do

00:57:05.760 --> 00:57:07.200
and there are a number of things

00:57:07.200 --> 00:57:09.390
that you are going to want to avoid doing

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:11.220
in order to optimize your sleep.

00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:13.890
First of all, you're
going to want to avoid

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:18.060
bright artificial lights of any color.

00:57:18.060 --> 00:57:20.040
Yes, of any color.

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:21.720
We haven't talked a lot
about blue blockers,

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:23.820
you know, lenses that
block blue wavelengths

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:26.190
or short wavelengths of light.

00:57:26.190 --> 00:57:27.840
I don't have anything
against blue blockers.

00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:30.570
In fact, many people find that
blue blockers provide them

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:34.050
some relief from headache
and some eye strain

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:35.010
if they wear blue blockers

00:57:35.010 --> 00:57:36.450
throughout the day and certainly at night.

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:37.890
But you don't need them,

00:57:37.890 --> 00:57:40.050
and even if you do wear them,

00:57:40.050 --> 00:57:42.390
you will find that if
lights are very bright,

00:57:42.390 --> 00:57:43.560
doesn't matter if it's a blue light,

00:57:43.560 --> 00:57:45.330
a yellow light, or a red light,

00:57:45.330 --> 00:57:48.660
those bright lights will
wake up your brain and body.

00:57:48.660 --> 00:57:51.060
They will activate the same mechanisms

00:57:51.060 --> 00:57:53.490
that were activated early
in the day by sunlight.

00:57:53.490 --> 00:57:56.520
However, and here's the
really diabolical twist,

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:57.353
I mentioned this earlier,

00:57:57.353 --> 00:57:58.680
but the diabolical twist

00:57:58.680 --> 00:58:01.260
in the way that your brain
and body respond to light

00:58:01.260 --> 00:58:03.240
is that early in the day,

00:58:03.240 --> 00:58:04.980
in the morning hours, you
need a lot of bright light,

00:58:04.980 --> 00:58:06.090
ideally from sunlight,

00:58:06.090 --> 00:58:08.550
to be very alert and to wake up,

00:58:08.550 --> 00:58:10.950
but in the evening hours
and nighttime hours,

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:14.550
it takes very little
light, very few photons,

00:58:14.550 --> 00:58:17.070
in order to wake up your brain and body

00:58:17.070 --> 00:58:20.370
and to disrupt your circadian
clock and disrupt your sleep.

00:58:20.370 --> 00:58:23.433
So what that means is that
once the sun goes down,

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:26.370
which, of course, is going to happen

00:58:26.370 --> 00:58:28.920
at different times of year
in different places on earth,

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:30.600
but once the sun goes down,

00:58:30.600 --> 00:58:32.940
you would be wise to
try and dim the lights

00:58:32.940 --> 00:58:35.490
in your indoor environment
most days, right?

00:58:35.490 --> 00:58:36.900
I realize some nights you're
going to throw a party

00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:37.950
and have people over.

00:58:37.950 --> 00:58:38.970
You might not want to dim the lights.

00:58:38.970 --> 00:58:40.320
Some nights you're going to go out,

00:58:40.320 --> 00:58:41.550
you might view a lot of bright lights.

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:43.530
But most nights of your life,

00:58:43.530 --> 00:58:45.150
you're going to want to dim the lights

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:48.173
in your internal environment.

00:58:48.173 --> 00:58:50.610
And ideally, the lights that you do use

00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:52.830
you would place low in
that physical environment.

00:58:52.830 --> 00:58:55.020
So you would try and
not use overhead lights,

00:58:55.020 --> 00:58:57.510
but rather rely on desk lamps or lights

00:58:57.510 --> 00:59:00.003
even placed low to the
floor, even on the floor.

00:59:00.960 --> 00:59:03.360
If you are going to use light at night,

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:04.920
and most people do,

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:07.530
I would encourage you to use
as little artificial light

00:59:07.530 --> 00:59:10.290
as is required to carry
out the activities you need

00:59:10.290 --> 00:59:11.820
to require safely.

00:59:11.820 --> 00:59:12.653
That could be studying,

00:59:12.653 --> 00:59:14.430
in which case you might
need a little bit more light

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:16.050
in order to read or study.

00:59:16.050 --> 00:59:18.150
If you're watching a television show

00:59:18.150 --> 00:59:19.920
or you're watching
something on your computer,

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:21.510
dim that screen way, way down,

00:59:21.510 --> 00:59:24.180
as dim as possible while
still, of course, being able

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:25.560
to view what you need to view.

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:27.810
Even better, I should say, ideally,

00:59:27.810 --> 00:59:30.150
you would use candlelight
and/or moonlight.

00:59:30.150 --> 00:59:31.890
Now, some nights the moon is really bright

00:59:31.890 --> 00:59:33.660
and you actually can use moonlight

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:35.370
to go about your usual activities.

00:59:35.370 --> 00:59:37.920
Moonlight might seem very, very bright,

00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:42.750
but, actually, moonlight is
fairly low light intensity,

00:59:42.750 --> 00:59:45.390
and candlelight, which
can also seem very bright,

00:59:45.390 --> 00:59:47.850
actually is very low light intensity.

00:59:47.850 --> 00:59:49.950
If you're sitting across a table

00:59:49.950 --> 00:59:50.880
with some candlelight there

00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:52.710
and it's a really bright candle,

00:59:52.710 --> 00:59:55.950
chances are it's only
about three to 10 lux,

00:59:55.950 --> 00:59:58.590
which is very, very little light energy

00:59:58.590 --> 01:00:00.780
compared to, say, an artificial desk lamp

01:00:00.780 --> 01:00:01.613
or an overhead light,

01:00:01.613 --> 01:00:02.670
which is going to be in the area

01:00:02.670 --> 01:00:05.640
of anywhere from a
hundred to a thousand lux.

01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:07.410
So candlelight is fine.

01:00:07.410 --> 01:00:09.930
Of course, be cautious with open flame,

01:00:09.930 --> 01:00:11.190
but candlelight is fine.

01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:12.210
Moonlight is fine.

01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:14.310
Dimming artificial lights is fine

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:16.230
provided they're dimmed way, way down.

01:00:16.230 --> 01:00:20.400
And again, try and avoid using
overhead artificial lights.

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:21.570
The absolute worst lights

01:00:21.570 --> 01:00:23.120
are going to be overhead
fluorescent lights

01:00:23.120 --> 01:00:26.040
of the sort that you would
have in the supermarket

01:00:26.040 --> 01:00:28.470
or that you would see at a gas station

01:00:28.470 --> 01:00:29.550
or something of that sort.

01:00:29.550 --> 01:00:33.570
And I confess, there are times
in which I'm driving home

01:00:33.570 --> 01:00:35.760
and it's late at night and I
want to be able to get to sleep

01:00:35.760 --> 01:00:37.740
and I'll need to stop at the grocery store

01:00:37.740 --> 01:00:39.060
or a gas station or something like that,

01:00:39.060 --> 01:00:40.950
I've actually put on sunglasses at night

01:00:40.950 --> 01:00:42.420
in order to avoid getting

01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:45.090
that bright light exposure at night.

01:00:45.090 --> 01:00:46.800
Although that's a little bit extreme,

01:00:46.800 --> 01:00:48.390
I have done that from time to time

01:00:48.390 --> 01:00:50.040
because that bright light exposure

01:00:50.040 --> 01:00:52.860
will absolutely quash, it will eliminate,

01:00:52.860 --> 01:00:55.530
any melatonin that
happens to be circulating

01:00:55.530 --> 01:00:56.363
in your brain and body.

01:00:56.363 --> 01:00:58.560
Now, melatonin, a lot of people
think of it as a supplement,

01:00:58.560 --> 01:01:00.810
but melatonin is naturally released

01:01:00.810 --> 01:01:03.750
as the evening comes about
and into the nighttime hours.

01:01:03.750 --> 01:01:05.760
It's the hormone that
makes you feel sleepy

01:01:05.760 --> 01:01:07.440
and allows you to fall asleep.

01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:09.150
So viewing bright light

01:01:09.150 --> 01:01:11.010
in the late evening
hours and nighttime hours

01:01:11.010 --> 01:01:14.490
is really not good for your sleep quality

01:01:14.490 --> 01:01:17.010
and your ability to fall and stay asleep.

01:01:17.010 --> 01:01:19.200
So for most people, a simple rule of thumb

01:01:19.200 --> 01:01:23.100
is going to be avoid bright
artificial lights of all colors,

01:01:23.100 --> 01:01:25.200
and in particular, overhead
bright artificial lights,

01:01:25.200 --> 01:01:27.630
between the hours of
10:00 p.m. and 4:00 a.m.

01:01:27.630 --> 01:01:29.130
That's right, between
10:00 p.m. and 4:00 a.m.,

01:01:29.130 --> 01:01:32.280
avoid those bright artificial
lights as much as possible.

01:01:32.280 --> 01:01:35.550
Use only as much light as
is absolutely necessary

01:01:35.550 --> 01:01:37.950
in order to carry out the
routines and activities

01:01:37.950 --> 01:01:39.840
you need to carry out safely.

01:01:39.840 --> 01:01:40.673
I should mention

01:01:40.673 --> 01:01:42.930
that the reason overhead
lights are problematic

01:01:42.930 --> 01:01:45.660
is the same reason why
sunlight is so great

01:01:45.660 --> 01:01:46.493
early in the day,

01:01:46.493 --> 01:01:49.050
which is that the cells,
that is, the neurons,

01:01:49.050 --> 01:01:51.480
that can wake up your brain and body

01:01:51.480 --> 01:01:53.430
through activation of the circadian clock

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:56.220
reside mainly in the bottom half or 2/3

01:01:56.220 --> 01:01:57.180
of your neural retina

01:01:57.180 --> 01:01:59.220
and the way the optics of your eyes work

01:01:59.220 --> 01:02:01.530
is that the cells on the
bottom half of your eye

01:02:01.530 --> 01:02:02.940
view the upper visual field.

01:02:02.940 --> 01:02:05.850
So this is a beautiful adaptive mechanism

01:02:05.850 --> 01:02:07.080
that allows these cells

01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:09.400
to respond to overhead light from sunlight

01:02:11.040 --> 01:02:13.170
in the early part of the
day and throughout the day.

01:02:13.170 --> 01:02:15.717
But in the evening, if you have
bright artificial lights on

01:02:15.717 --> 01:02:18.300
and those bright artificial
lights are overhead lights,

01:02:18.300 --> 01:02:20.790
it's going to more closely mimic

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:23.400
what sunlight does in the evening time,

01:02:23.400 --> 01:02:24.690
and that turns out to be a bad thing

01:02:24.690 --> 01:02:26.640
if your goal is to eventually go to sleep.

01:02:26.640 --> 01:02:29.064
So again, do like the Scandinavians do.

01:02:29.064 --> 01:02:32.100
Use lights that are set
low in the room at night,

01:02:32.100 --> 01:02:35.100
and if you really want to
optimize your sleep-wake cycles,

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:37.470
I suppose you could also do the
opposite throughout the day.

01:02:37.470 --> 01:02:38.820
You could really emphasize the use

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:41.220
of bright artificial lights and sunlight

01:02:41.220 --> 01:02:42.053
that comes from above.

01:02:42.053 --> 01:02:43.860
And of course, sunlight
always comes from above.

01:02:43.860 --> 01:02:48.600
But if you're working in
a given office environment

01:02:48.600 --> 01:02:50.220
and, you know, it's 2:00 p.m. or 3:00 p.m.

01:02:50.220 --> 01:02:52.080
and you want to be as awake as possible,

01:02:52.080 --> 01:02:52.913
really crank up the overhead lights.

01:02:52.913 --> 01:02:54.810
And then in the evening,

01:02:54.810 --> 01:02:57.090
which is this critical period
three that we're referring to,

01:02:57.090 --> 01:03:00.090
really try and dim those
lights or have them off

01:03:00.090 --> 01:03:01.830
or just rely on candlelight or moonlight

01:03:01.830 --> 01:03:05.280
from the hours of about
10:00 p.m. until 4:00 a.m.

01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:06.570
Our good friend Samer Hattar,

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:08.100
who's been on this podcast before,

01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:10.170
Samer is director of
the chronobiology unit

01:03:10.170 --> 01:03:12.210
at the National Institutes
of Mental Health,

01:03:12.210 --> 01:03:15.180
well, he's absolutely obsessive
about this light stuff

01:03:15.180 --> 01:03:16.110
and avoiding light at night.

01:03:16.110 --> 01:03:19.290
In fact, he lives in what I sort of joke

01:03:19.290 --> 01:03:20.460
is like a cave at night.

01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:22.350
From 9:00 p.m. until 5:00 a.m.,

01:03:22.350 --> 01:03:25.380
which is really his kind of sleep cycle,

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:27.330
he has his house so dark

01:03:27.330 --> 01:03:28.320
that you'd be lucky to be able

01:03:28.320 --> 01:03:29.940
to find a spoon in the kitchen.

01:03:29.940 --> 01:03:30.773
In fact, you'd be lucky

01:03:30.773 --> 01:03:32.550
to find your way down
the hallway if you're me.

01:03:32.550 --> 01:03:34.350
But in any case, dim the lights.

01:03:34.350 --> 01:03:35.610
Turn them way, way down.

01:03:35.610 --> 01:03:36.540
It will serve you well.

01:03:36.540 --> 01:03:38.760
It will make it much easier for you

01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:41.790
to get sleepy and stay
sleepy and fall asleep

01:03:41.790 --> 01:03:43.560
and stay asleep throughout the night.

01:03:43.560 --> 01:03:46.440
Now, not to depart from
this critical period three,

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:50.433
but if you recall, viewing
that afternoon light,

01:03:51.420 --> 01:03:52.860
the low solar angle light

01:03:52.860 --> 01:03:54.750
as the sun is heading down in the sky,

01:03:54.750 --> 01:03:55.830
so it could be sunset

01:03:55.830 --> 01:03:59.490
or what I call circa
sunset, around sunset,

01:03:59.490 --> 01:04:01.680
well, doing that is going to slightly,

01:04:01.680 --> 01:04:04.500
but not completely, offset
any of the negative effects

01:04:04.500 --> 01:04:06.810
of viewing artificial light at night.

01:04:06.810 --> 01:04:08.490
So I don't want to give
people a pass here,

01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:09.840
but let's say you know

01:04:09.840 --> 01:04:13.020
that you're going to watch
some Netflix at night

01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:14.910
or you're going to be up late studying

01:04:14.910 --> 01:04:17.550
and yet you still want to be
able to fall and stay asleep.

01:04:17.550 --> 01:04:20.070
Definitely make sure you
see that evening light.

01:04:20.070 --> 01:04:21.300
There's a great study.

01:04:21.300 --> 01:04:22.530
We'll provide a link to this study,

01:04:22.530 --> 01:04:26.850
which showed that if people
view evening sunset light

01:04:26.850 --> 01:04:27.870
or evening sunsets

01:04:27.870 --> 01:04:30.030
or sunlight right around
the time of sunset,

01:04:30.030 --> 01:04:33.750
it really serves to
inoculate or offset some,

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:35.730
again, some, not all,
of the negative effects

01:04:35.730 --> 01:04:36.563
of artificial light

01:04:36.563 --> 01:04:38.700
between the hours of
10:00 p.m. and 4:00 a.m.

01:04:38.700 --> 01:04:39.533
Now, that's light.

01:04:39.533 --> 01:04:40.410
But as you recall,

01:04:40.410 --> 01:04:43.170
we also have this tool
related to temperature,

01:04:43.170 --> 01:04:46.140
and you're probably not
going to be surprised

01:04:46.140 --> 01:04:48.690
that the way to leverage
temperature in the evening

01:04:48.690 --> 01:04:49.770
is the exact opposite

01:04:49.770 --> 01:04:51.690
of the way that you want
to leverage temperature

01:04:51.690 --> 01:04:52.523
early in the day.

01:04:52.523 --> 01:04:54.300
Early in the day, temperature increases

01:04:54.300 --> 01:04:57.240
from cold showers or exercise,
et cetera, wake you up.

01:04:57.240 --> 01:04:59.970
What that means is that taking
a cold shower late at night

01:04:59.970 --> 01:05:01.890
is probably a bad idea.

01:05:01.890 --> 01:05:05.043
Rather, taking a nice hot bath or a sauna,

01:05:05.970 --> 01:05:07.680
you might think would heat up your body,

01:05:07.680 --> 01:05:08.700
and indeed, that's what happens

01:05:08.700 --> 01:05:10.320
if you stay in a very long time,

01:05:10.320 --> 01:05:12.690
but if you do hot tub or a hot bath

01:05:12.690 --> 01:05:13.740
or a sauna in the evening,

01:05:13.740 --> 01:05:15.930
and you don't stay in for
more than 20 or 30 minutes

01:05:15.930 --> 01:05:16.763
and you get out,

01:05:16.763 --> 01:05:20.340
you take maybe a cool-ish
shower or a warm shower,

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:23.370
then what happens is there's
a compensatory cooling off

01:05:23.370 --> 01:05:24.630
of your core body temperature

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:26.070
for the reasons we discussed earlier,

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:29.100
and your body temperature will
drop by one to three degrees

01:05:29.100 --> 01:05:31.530
and it will make it much
easier to get into sleep.

01:05:31.530 --> 01:05:32.363
So if you're somebody

01:05:32.363 --> 01:05:35.730
that enjoys hot baths,
hot showers, or hot tubs,

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:36.780
evening and nighttime

01:05:36.780 --> 01:05:38.100
is going to be the best time to do that

01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:40.410
if your goal is to facilitate sleep.

01:05:40.410 --> 01:05:41.940
Similarly, you should try

01:05:41.940 --> 01:05:45.480
and make your sleeping environment
pretty cool, if not cold.

01:05:45.480 --> 01:05:46.830
Now, that doesn't mean you need to be cold

01:05:46.830 --> 01:05:47.970
while you're asleep.

01:05:47.970 --> 01:05:49.830
You can get under as many
blankets as you need,

01:05:49.830 --> 01:05:52.740
but it's a good idea to make
your sleeping environment cool.

01:05:52.740 --> 01:05:55.020
In fact, drop the temperature
in that sleeping environment

01:05:55.020 --> 01:05:56.970
by at least three degrees

01:05:56.970 --> 01:05:58.740
and you'll be happy that you did.

01:05:58.740 --> 01:06:01.140
Now, some people rely on
things like Eight Sleep.

01:06:01.140 --> 01:06:01.973
I use that.

01:06:01.973 --> 01:06:05.070
One of these controllable
temperature mattress covers.

01:06:05.070 --> 01:06:06.480
Other people would simply do this

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:09.270
by putting a fan in the
room or opening a window.

01:06:09.270 --> 01:06:10.410
Again, depends on time of year,

01:06:10.410 --> 01:06:12.660
depends on technology,
depends on budgets, et cetera.

01:06:12.660 --> 01:06:13.890
But you're going to want to sleep

01:06:13.890 --> 01:06:16.650
in a relatively cool or
cold sleeping environment

01:06:16.650 --> 01:06:20.880
and then layer on the blankets
as needed to stay asleep.

01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:23.040
And I say as needed
because one of the things

01:06:23.040 --> 01:06:25.590
that you're going to do in your sleep,

01:06:25.590 --> 01:06:27.120
or if you happen to wake up,

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:30.210
is if you're too warm, you're
going to put a foot or a hand

01:06:30.210 --> 01:06:32.250
out from under those blankets.

01:06:32.250 --> 01:06:34.470
And the reason for doing
that is very logical

01:06:34.470 --> 01:06:35.970
once you understand the mechanism.

01:06:35.970 --> 01:06:37.680
You have special portals,

01:06:37.680 --> 01:06:40.560
you essentially have ways of passing heat,

01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:43.110
excuse me, in and out of your body

01:06:43.110 --> 01:06:44.880
primarily through the palms of your hands,

01:06:44.880 --> 01:06:45.900
the upper half of your face,

01:06:45.900 --> 01:06:47.220
and the bottoms of your feet

01:06:47.220 --> 01:06:48.540
through so-called glabrous skin.

01:06:48.540 --> 01:06:50.880
This was covered in the
episode with Dr. Craig Heller

01:06:50.880 --> 01:06:52.880
from the biology department at Stanford.

01:06:53.790 --> 01:06:55.890
If you lower the temperature
in your sleeping environment,

01:06:55.890 --> 01:06:57.510
so lower the temperature in that room

01:06:57.510 --> 01:07:00.480
or use a controllable mattress
cover that can cool down

01:07:00.480 --> 01:07:02.310
like Eight Sleep or
something of that sort,

01:07:02.310 --> 01:07:05.670
it's naturally going to make
your sleep environment cooler,

01:07:05.670 --> 01:07:07.770
and if you're too warm under the blankets,

01:07:07.770 --> 01:07:09.660
all you have to do is
extend a hand or a foot

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:11.040
out from under those blankets.

01:07:11.040 --> 01:07:12.840
Whereas if the sleeping
environment that you're in

01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:14.040
is too warm,

01:07:14.040 --> 01:07:16.050
there's very little you can do to cool off

01:07:16.050 --> 01:07:17.790
besides push off those blankets.

01:07:17.790 --> 01:07:19.020
So for instance, if you're too warm

01:07:19.020 --> 01:07:20.340
and you're waking up in
the middle of the night,

01:07:20.340 --> 01:07:22.140
which is what happens if you get too warm,

01:07:22.140 --> 01:07:23.250
you'll push off those blankets.

01:07:23.250 --> 01:07:24.900
But if the room is too warm,

01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:25.733
well, what are you going to do?

01:07:25.733 --> 01:07:27.720
You'd probably have to put
your hands into some cool water

01:07:27.720 --> 01:07:29.820
or take a coolish shower or something

01:07:29.820 --> 01:07:30.960
for a couple of seconds.

01:07:30.960 --> 01:07:32.340
That's not very practical.

01:07:32.340 --> 01:07:35.190
Better to just keep the
sleeping environment cool.

01:07:35.190 --> 01:07:37.800
I'm not a big fan of
people putting socks on

01:07:37.800 --> 01:07:38.633
while they sleep,

01:07:38.633 --> 01:07:40.290
or I should say, I'm not
a fan of putting socks on

01:07:40.290 --> 01:07:41.123
while I sleep,

01:07:41.123 --> 01:07:43.350
because that eliminates
this glabrous skin portal

01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:44.850
on the bottoms of one's feet.

01:07:44.850 --> 01:07:45.960
So for those of you that have heard,

01:07:45.960 --> 01:07:47.220
you know, wear socks while you sleep,

01:07:47.220 --> 01:07:48.660
that works great for people

01:07:48.660 --> 01:07:50.910
that tend to run too cold while they sleep

01:07:50.910 --> 01:07:52.680
and wake up because their feet get cold,

01:07:52.680 --> 01:07:53.850
but if you're somebody who wakes up

01:07:53.850 --> 01:07:54.683
in the middle of the night,

01:07:54.683 --> 01:07:55.516
chances are you're waking up

01:07:55.516 --> 01:07:57.930
because you're getting too warm

01:07:57.930 --> 01:07:59.070
and the best thing that you could do

01:07:59.070 --> 01:08:01.050
is to cool or lower the temperature

01:08:01.050 --> 01:08:01.920
in the room that you're sleeping

01:08:01.920 --> 01:08:03.030
and not wear socks,

01:08:03.030 --> 01:08:05.700
get under as many blankets
as you need to fall asleep,

01:08:05.700 --> 01:08:06.870
and then across the night,

01:08:06.870 --> 01:08:08.640
you'll naturally just
move a hand or a foot

01:08:08.640 --> 01:08:10.770
or all hands and feet out
from under those blankets

01:08:10.770 --> 01:08:11.910
to cool off

01:08:11.910 --> 01:08:14.400
because of the relationship
between temperature and sleep.

01:08:14.400 --> 01:08:16.590
That is, dropping your
core body temperature

01:08:16.590 --> 01:08:17.820
one to three degrees

01:08:17.820 --> 01:08:19.950
gets you into sleep and
helps you stay asleep.

01:08:19.950 --> 01:08:21.870
So let's say you do
exercise late in the day

01:08:21.870 --> 01:08:23.760
and you're finding yourself
very alert in the evening

01:08:23.760 --> 01:08:25.350
and you need to fall asleep,

01:08:25.350 --> 01:08:26.730
or let's say you've exercised

01:08:26.730 --> 01:08:29.310
and you needed four cups of espresso

01:08:29.310 --> 01:08:30.990
in order to do that exercise.

01:08:30.990 --> 01:08:32.070
Well, there are a few
things that you can do

01:08:32.070 --> 01:08:34.170
to try and bring your nervous system down

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:35.820
into more state of calmness,

01:08:35.820 --> 01:08:36.653
and you can do that

01:08:36.653 --> 01:08:39.180
also by lowering your
core body temperature.

01:08:39.180 --> 01:08:40.740
One of those I already
talked about before,

01:08:40.740 --> 01:08:43.200
taking a nice hot shower or a hot bath

01:08:43.200 --> 01:08:44.490
and then getting out and cooling off

01:08:44.490 --> 01:08:46.320
will decrease your body temperature.

01:08:46.320 --> 01:08:48.540
Maybe not enough to get you into sleep

01:08:48.540 --> 01:08:51.000
if you have a ton of
caffeine in your system.

01:08:51.000 --> 01:08:54.900
But again, you can use this
mechanism of temperature shifts

01:08:54.900 --> 01:08:56.130
to wake up

01:08:56.130 --> 01:08:57.630
or temperature shifts to fall asleep

01:08:57.630 --> 01:08:59.730
in ways that really can help you overcome

01:08:59.730 --> 01:09:03.570
some of the irregularities
in your sleep-wake cycle

01:09:03.570 --> 01:09:05.460
and exercise cycle, et cetera.

01:09:05.460 --> 01:09:07.320
Because, of course, nobody's perfect.

01:09:07.320 --> 01:09:09.540
Some days we end up having
to workout in the afternoon

01:09:09.540 --> 01:09:11.340
or we'll miss the workout entirely.

01:09:11.340 --> 01:09:13.230
Other days, we end up
having that cup of coffee

01:09:13.230 --> 01:09:14.160
in the afternoon with a friend

01:09:14.160 --> 01:09:15.960
and then we have a hard
time falling asleep.

01:09:15.960 --> 01:09:17.220
So you can use these tools

01:09:17.220 --> 01:09:18.794
not just in their optimized form,

01:09:18.794 --> 01:09:21.450
you know, being absolutely
obsessive and compulsive

01:09:21.450 --> 01:09:23.640
about exactly when you
do each of those tools.

01:09:23.640 --> 01:09:27.090
That would be wonderful,
but life happens as they say

01:09:27.090 --> 01:09:29.361
and some days you're going
to feel too alert at night

01:09:29.361 --> 01:09:30.420
and you want to fall asleep,

01:09:30.420 --> 01:09:33.420
or you've got to get up
especially early the next morning

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:34.253
and you're not somebody

01:09:34.253 --> 01:09:35.790
who normally goes to bed at 10:00 p.m.

01:09:35.790 --> 01:09:37.950
Well, that's when a something
like a hot bath or a sauna

01:09:37.950 --> 01:09:38.783
can really benefit you

01:09:38.783 --> 01:09:41.100
because it can adjust your
temperature rhythm accordingly.

01:09:41.100 --> 01:09:44.040
I would be remiss if I
didn't touch on alcohol

01:09:44.040 --> 01:09:46.080
and CBD and THC.

01:09:46.080 --> 01:09:48.000
I always get questions about these.

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:49.890
And I should say, of course,

01:09:49.890 --> 01:09:52.980
many places, but not all, THC is illegal.

01:09:52.980 --> 01:09:54.360
Although, there are medical uses

01:09:54.360 --> 01:09:56.970
and in some places, it's decriminalized,

01:09:56.970 --> 01:09:58.830
other places it's legal.

01:09:58.830 --> 01:10:00.810
Alcohol, of course, is consumed

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:03.570
almost as frequently as
caffeine is consumed.

01:10:03.570 --> 01:10:04.860
I personally don't drink alcohol.

01:10:04.860 --> 01:10:07.140
I don't have anything against it per se.

01:10:07.140 --> 01:10:09.120
I just don't tend to enjoy it.

01:10:09.120 --> 01:10:10.280
One of the reasons I don't enjoy it

01:10:10.280 --> 01:10:12.930
is if I drink alcohol,
I simply fall asleep.

01:10:12.930 --> 01:10:14.610
So that doesn't really accomplish

01:10:14.610 --> 01:10:16.590
any of the things that I
really want to accomplish

01:10:16.590 --> 01:10:20.130
because the sleep that one
gets after drinking alcohol

01:10:20.130 --> 01:10:22.350
is greatly disrupted sleep.

01:10:22.350 --> 01:10:24.810
Hate to break it to you,
but that's the truth.

01:10:24.810 --> 01:10:27.450
And when Dr. Matt Walker
came on this podcast,

01:10:27.450 --> 01:10:29.100
he said exactly the same thing.

01:10:29.100 --> 01:10:34.100
While THC and alcohol do
help some people fall asleep

01:10:35.310 --> 01:10:36.360
and maybe even stay asleep,

01:10:36.360 --> 01:10:39.480
the architecture of
that sleep is suboptimal

01:10:39.480 --> 01:10:40.710
compared to the sleep they would get

01:10:40.710 --> 01:10:43.080
without alcohol or THC in their system.

01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:45.630
So I'm not here to tell you
what to do or not to do.

01:10:45.630 --> 01:10:48.870
I'm certainly not the substance police.

01:10:48.870 --> 01:10:50.010
That's not my role.

01:10:50.010 --> 01:10:51.570
I'm just reporting to you the biology.

01:10:51.570 --> 01:10:54.660
If your sleep is not restoring you

01:10:54.660 --> 01:10:56.670
to the extent that you feel it should,

01:10:56.670 --> 01:10:59.910
or if you are regularly
relying on a drink or two

01:10:59.910 --> 01:11:01.790
in order to fall asleep,

01:11:01.790 --> 01:11:04.500
or THC in order to fall asleep,

01:11:04.500 --> 01:11:07.350
that is disrupting your
total pattern of sleep.

01:11:07.350 --> 01:11:08.730
However, I do realize

01:11:08.730 --> 01:11:12.960
that nowadays a lot of people
are relying on THC and/or CBD,

01:11:12.960 --> 01:11:14.100
especially edible forms,

01:11:14.100 --> 01:11:15.767
in order to fall and stay asleep.

01:11:15.767 --> 01:11:18.570
And, you know, we can
just acknowledge the data.

01:11:18.570 --> 01:11:21.480
It does seem that there's
an anxiety lowering effect

01:11:21.480 --> 01:11:22.830
of some of those compounds

01:11:22.830 --> 01:11:24.270
that do help people

01:11:24.270 --> 01:11:26.580
who have a hard time
falling and staying asleep

01:11:26.580 --> 01:11:28.860
because of reasons related to anxiety.

01:11:28.860 --> 01:11:29.693
Although, in a moment,

01:11:29.693 --> 01:11:33.180
we'll talk about some supplements
and supplement protocols

01:11:33.180 --> 01:11:36.150
that can also assist in the
ability to fall and stay asleep

01:11:36.150 --> 01:11:37.650
and that can adjust anxiety

01:11:37.650 --> 01:11:40.230
and that do not seem to
disrupt sleep architecture

01:11:40.230 --> 01:11:41.063
in negative ways

01:11:41.063 --> 01:11:43.500
and, in fact, can enhance
the depth and quality

01:11:43.500 --> 01:11:44.820
of sleep architecture.

01:11:44.820 --> 01:11:47.580
Okay, so you've done everything
correctly up until now.

01:11:47.580 --> 01:11:49.663
You got your morning routine
from critical period one.

01:11:49.663 --> 01:11:51.510
You've got your afternoon routine.

01:11:51.510 --> 01:11:53.580
You saw some sunlight in the afternoon.

01:11:53.580 --> 01:11:55.320
You avoided caffeine in the eight hours

01:11:55.320 --> 01:11:57.090
or 10 hours before bedtime.

01:11:57.090 --> 01:11:58.470
You're not drinking alcohol.

01:11:58.470 --> 01:12:00.420
You've cooled down the room.

01:12:00.420 --> 01:12:01.530
You're doing all these things right.

01:12:01.530 --> 01:12:04.170
You've dimmed the lights,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:12:04.170 --> 01:12:06.690
What else can we do in
order to optimize our sleep?

01:12:06.690 --> 01:12:10.590
Well, I always say behavioral tools first,

01:12:10.590 --> 01:12:11.850
then look to nutrition,

01:12:11.850 --> 01:12:14.520
then, if necessary,
look to supplementation,

01:12:14.520 --> 01:12:18.810
and then, if still necessary,
look to prescription drugs

01:12:18.810 --> 01:12:21.990
obviously prescribed by a
board certified physician.

01:12:21.990 --> 01:12:24.180
Well, we've talked a lot
about the behavioral tools

01:12:24.180 --> 01:12:25.650
for critical period three.

01:12:25.650 --> 01:12:26.910
We have not talked a lot

01:12:26.910 --> 01:12:28.920
about the supplementation-based tools.

01:12:28.920 --> 01:12:30.960
There are supplements that for most people

01:12:30.960 --> 01:12:35.070
will greatly improve their
ability to fall and stay asleep

01:12:35.070 --> 01:12:38.430
and the three main
supplements in that category

01:12:38.430 --> 01:12:40.800
or that kit of sleep supplements,

01:12:40.800 --> 01:12:42.060
and I've talked about these before,

01:12:42.060 --> 01:12:47.060
are magnesium threonate,
so T-H-R-E-O-N-A-T-E,

01:12:48.210 --> 01:12:51.930
apigenin, A-P-I-G-E-N-I-N, apigenin,

01:12:51.930 --> 01:12:55.380
and theanine, T-H-E-A-N-I-N-E, theanine.

01:12:55.380 --> 01:12:56.970
Now, some important things to point out

01:12:56.970 --> 01:13:00.300
about mag threonate, as it's
called, apigenin, and theanine.

01:13:00.300 --> 01:13:01.500
First of all,

01:13:01.500 --> 01:13:03.510
you don't necessarily
need to take all three,

01:13:03.510 --> 01:13:05.940
although, many people
get a synergistic effect

01:13:05.940 --> 01:13:07.170
from taking all three.

01:13:07.170 --> 01:13:10.230
In fact, you may not even
need to take even one.

01:13:10.230 --> 01:13:13.050
What I recommend is that
if you're already doing

01:13:13.050 --> 01:13:15.660
all the behavioral tools regularly

01:13:15.660 --> 01:13:17.910
and you're still having
trouble falling asleep

01:13:17.910 --> 01:13:19.440
and staying asleep,

01:13:19.440 --> 01:13:23.040
well, then you might try
one of the supplements

01:13:23.040 --> 01:13:24.150
within this sleep stack.

01:13:24.150 --> 01:13:26.190
They do have fairly
wide margins for safety.

01:13:26.190 --> 01:13:28.530
Although, I should also say,

01:13:28.530 --> 01:13:30.630
anytime you're going to
add or remove something

01:13:30.630 --> 01:13:33.090
from your supplement protocol
or your nutritional program,

01:13:33.090 --> 01:13:34.590
you definitely want to
talk to your physician.

01:13:34.590 --> 01:13:35.760
I don't just say that to protect us.

01:13:35.760 --> 01:13:37.140
I say that to protect you.

01:13:37.140 --> 01:13:39.300
But for most people, the margins
of safety on these things

01:13:39.300 --> 01:13:40.590
are going to be pretty broad.

01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:42.960
A couple of notes about dosages.

01:13:42.960 --> 01:13:45.540
For some people, the dosages
of any one or several

01:13:45.540 --> 01:13:47.820
of the supplements I
mentioned will be zero.

01:13:47.820 --> 01:13:48.930
That is, you won't need them

01:13:48.930 --> 01:13:53.220
in order to get and stay asleep
most nights of your life.

01:13:53.220 --> 01:13:55.230
That's terrific if you don't need them.

01:13:55.230 --> 01:13:56.670
For many people, however,

01:13:56.670 --> 01:14:01.170
taking 145 milligrams
of magnesium threonate

01:14:01.170 --> 01:14:02.490
can be very beneficial.

01:14:02.490 --> 01:14:06.240
That's the dosage that most
people will benefit from.

01:14:06.240 --> 01:14:07.530
Some people need to go a little higher.

01:14:07.530 --> 01:14:09.510
Some people need to go a little bit lower.

01:14:09.510 --> 01:14:11.160
One of the reasons that
we've been pointing people

01:14:11.160 --> 01:14:14.760
towards single-ingredient
formulations these days

01:14:14.760 --> 01:14:16.140
is because it allows people

01:14:16.140 --> 01:14:18.630
to adjust the dosage of one component

01:14:18.630 --> 01:14:19.770
of a so-called sleep stack

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:23.610
without having to disrupt the
dosage of another component,

01:14:23.610 --> 01:14:24.443
and so on.

01:14:24.443 --> 01:14:25.560
It also allows people to try

01:14:25.560 --> 01:14:28.320
just one element within the sleep stack

01:14:28.320 --> 01:14:30.960
without having to purchase
and try the others,

01:14:30.960 --> 01:14:32.520
which is a problem if
you're buying a blend

01:14:32.520 --> 01:14:33.930
of a lot of different ingredients.

01:14:33.930 --> 01:14:37.080
So 145 milligrams of magnesium threonate.

01:14:37.080 --> 01:14:39.870
50, 5-0, milligrams of apigenin.

01:14:39.870 --> 01:14:42.540
And again, you could just
take the apigenin on its own.

01:14:42.540 --> 01:14:46.560
And 100 to 400 milligrams of theanine

01:14:46.560 --> 01:14:48.840
taken, again, alone or in combination

01:14:48.840 --> 01:14:51.360
with the other supplements
mentioned in the stack

01:14:51.360 --> 01:14:54.210
many people find allows
them to get really drowsy

01:14:54.210 --> 01:14:56.610
and fall asleep, sleep really deeply,

01:14:56.610 --> 01:14:58.770
and they feel much more
refreshed the next day

01:14:58.770 --> 01:15:01.110
and they don't have a grogginess to them.

01:15:01.110 --> 01:15:04.620
Now, a couple of notes about
these different supplements.

01:15:04.620 --> 01:15:07.650
About 5% of people report

01:15:07.650 --> 01:15:10.380
that magnesium threonate
really disrupts their gut.

01:15:10.380 --> 01:15:12.630
It gives them diarrhea
or gastric distress.

01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:14.430
In which case, don't take it.

01:15:14.430 --> 01:15:19.113
If magnesium threonate disrupts
your gut or your digestion

01:15:19.113 --> 01:15:21.210
to a point where it's
uncomfortable or at all

01:15:21.210 --> 01:15:23.040
and you don't like it,
don't take any of it.

01:15:23.040 --> 01:15:24.390
The proper dosage for you, in other words,

01:15:24.390 --> 01:15:26.130
would be zero milligrams.

01:15:26.130 --> 01:15:28.230
Now, in a slightly different way,

01:15:28.230 --> 01:15:31.260
many people who can
tolerate magnesium threonate

01:15:31.260 --> 01:15:33.780
or really thrive on magnesium threonate

01:15:33.780 --> 01:15:35.010
and like apigenin

01:15:35.010 --> 01:15:37.050
might find that theanine,

01:15:37.050 --> 01:15:39.150
even at the lowest dose
of a hundred milligrams,

01:15:39.150 --> 01:15:41.190
'cause, again, the range is
a hundred to 400 milligrams,

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:44.070
that theanine gives them such vivid dreams

01:15:44.070 --> 01:15:46.350
that they actually find it disruptive

01:15:46.350 --> 01:15:47.880
where they wake up in
the middle of the night

01:15:47.880 --> 01:15:49.800
or they find that the
sleep that they're getting

01:15:49.800 --> 01:15:51.360
is kind of anxiety ridden

01:15:51.360 --> 01:15:53.130
because of the intensity of those dreams.

01:15:53.130 --> 01:15:55.200
So some people might
choose to leave theanine

01:15:55.200 --> 01:15:56.370
out of the sleep stack

01:15:56.370 --> 01:15:58.227
and just take magnesium
threonate or apigenin

01:15:58.227 --> 01:16:00.510
And again, some people might
leave magnesium threonate

01:16:00.510 --> 01:16:01.660
out of the sleep stack.

01:16:02.760 --> 01:16:05.220
Again, all of this is really about finding

01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:08.040
the supplementation protocol
that's ideal for you.

01:16:08.040 --> 01:16:09.300
I should mention that whether or not

01:16:09.300 --> 01:16:11.790
you're taking one or two
or three of the components

01:16:11.790 --> 01:16:12.783
of the sleep stack,

01:16:13.620 --> 01:16:14.750
the ideal time to take those

01:16:14.750 --> 01:16:17.100
is 30 to 60 minutes before bedtime,

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:19.110
especially if you haven't
had anything to eat

01:16:19.110 --> 01:16:21.060
for the three hours or so before bedtime.

01:16:21.060 --> 01:16:22.530
I confess that oftentimes

01:16:22.530 --> 01:16:24.120
I'll have a little bit of a
snack late in the evening,

01:16:24.120 --> 01:16:25.560
some berries or something.

01:16:25.560 --> 01:16:27.420
I try not to eat too close to bedtime,

01:16:27.420 --> 01:16:29.310
but some evenings just
because of work schedule,

01:16:29.310 --> 01:16:31.290
I'll get home late, be 9:00 p.m.,

01:16:31.290 --> 01:16:32.340
and I'll eat a big meal

01:16:32.340 --> 01:16:34.410
and then I'll take the
sleep stack and fall asleep.

01:16:34.410 --> 01:16:36.420
Every once in a while,
that just so happens.

01:16:36.420 --> 01:16:39.270
Nobody's perfect. Certainly I'm not.

01:16:39.270 --> 01:16:41.880
But that sleep stack
can be very beneficial.

01:16:41.880 --> 01:16:44.670
And I do think that it's
preferable to melatonin.

01:16:44.670 --> 01:16:45.503
Here's the reason.

01:16:45.503 --> 01:16:47.280
First of all, melatonin is a hormone

01:16:47.280 --> 01:16:48.600
that you endogenously make.

01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:50.250
You now know a lot about melatonin

01:16:50.250 --> 01:16:52.020
and it's controlled by light,

01:16:52.020 --> 01:16:54.750
meaning light inhibits
it or eliminates it,

01:16:54.750 --> 01:16:56.043
darkness promotes it.

01:16:56.910 --> 01:17:01.110
And melatonin indeed
can help us fall asleep,

01:17:01.110 --> 01:17:02.700
but the dosages of melatonin

01:17:02.700 --> 01:17:04.440
that are contained in
most commercial products

01:17:04.440 --> 01:17:07.020
is far, far, far greater

01:17:07.020 --> 01:17:08.250
than what we would make endogenously.

01:17:08.250 --> 01:17:10.890
So it's really supraphysiological.

01:17:10.890 --> 01:17:11.820
So that's of concern

01:17:11.820 --> 01:17:14.460
because melatonin is not just responsible

01:17:14.460 --> 01:17:16.860
for making us sleepy and fall asleep.

01:17:16.860 --> 01:17:18.118
It also does things

01:17:18.118 --> 01:17:21.030
like interacts with other hormone systems,

01:17:21.030 --> 01:17:22.110
testosterone and estrogen,

01:17:22.110 --> 01:17:24.747
even in the puberty system, in kids.

01:17:24.747 --> 01:17:27.690
Is taking melatonin every
once in a while a problem

01:17:27.690 --> 01:17:29.670
for adjusting to jet lag, et cetera?

01:17:29.670 --> 01:17:31.830
Probably not. I would even say no.

01:17:31.830 --> 01:17:33.930
But taking it chronically over time,

01:17:33.930 --> 01:17:36.480
especially kids taking
it chronically over time,

01:17:36.480 --> 01:17:38.010
can potentially be problematic.

01:17:38.010 --> 01:17:40.230
So at least in my opinion,
these other supplements

01:17:40.230 --> 01:17:42.690
are going to be preferable to melatonin.

01:17:42.690 --> 01:17:44.730
Now, as I mentioned in the
beginning of today's episode,

01:17:44.730 --> 01:17:46.080
there are some other things

01:17:46.080 --> 01:17:47.939
that I certainly take
every once in a while

01:17:47.939 --> 01:17:50.729
and that other people
might consider taking

01:17:50.729 --> 01:17:53.910
in addition to the sleep
stack I talked about before

01:17:53.910 --> 01:17:55.740
or in place of that sleep stack

01:17:55.740 --> 01:17:57.570
if that sleep stack
doesn't work well for them.

01:17:57.570 --> 01:17:59.040
So every third or fourth night,

01:17:59.040 --> 01:18:01.710
I will take two grams of glycine

01:18:01.710 --> 01:18:03.690
and a hundred milligrams of GABA

01:18:03.690 --> 01:18:06.540
in addition to the standard sleep stack

01:18:06.540 --> 01:18:07.500
that I talked about before.

01:18:07.500 --> 01:18:09.801
So I'm taking mag threonate,
apigenin, and theanine,

01:18:09.801 --> 01:18:12.240
and then I will also take two
grams of glycine and GABA,

01:18:12.240 --> 01:18:16.080
which I find greatly enhances
my ability to get into sleep.

01:18:16.080 --> 01:18:19.410
But the reason I only add glycine and GABA

01:18:19.410 --> 01:18:21.000
every third or fourth night

01:18:21.000 --> 01:18:22.500
is that if I take it too often,

01:18:22.500 --> 01:18:24.090
I find that the entire sleep stack

01:18:24.090 --> 01:18:25.590
doesn't work quite as effectively.

01:18:25.590 --> 01:18:27.210
I don't know exactly why this is the case,

01:18:27.210 --> 01:18:29.910
but in any event, that's what I do.

01:18:29.910 --> 01:18:33.600
And more recently, I've
also started using inositol,

01:18:33.600 --> 01:18:35.760
in particular myo-inositol.

01:18:35.760 --> 01:18:39.330
Every other night, I'll take
900 milligrams of myo-inositol

01:18:39.330 --> 01:18:42.600
in addition to mag threonate,
apigenin, and theanine,

01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:46.620
and not on the nights when
I take glycine and GABA.

01:18:46.620 --> 01:18:48.570
So I'm adding 900 milligrams of inositol

01:18:48.570 --> 01:18:50.460
to the standard sleep stack

01:18:50.460 --> 01:18:52.890
of mag threonate, theanine, and apigenin.

01:18:52.890 --> 01:18:56.310
And what I find is not only
does it greatly enhance

01:18:56.310 --> 01:18:58.110
my ability to fall asleep quickly,

01:18:58.110 --> 01:18:59.790
but if I wake up in the
middle of the night,

01:18:59.790 --> 01:19:02.580
which I often do to use the bathroom,

01:19:02.580 --> 01:19:05.490
I find it very, very
easy to fall back asleep.

01:19:05.490 --> 01:19:10.290
Whereas when I don't take
inositol every other night or so,

01:19:10.290 --> 01:19:11.760
I find that if I wake up
in the middle of the night,

01:19:11.760 --> 01:19:14.370
it's a bit more of a
challenge to fall back asleep.

01:19:14.370 --> 01:19:16.950
So inositol has a number of different uses

01:19:16.950 --> 01:19:19.560
that have been discussed
in terms of mental health

01:19:19.560 --> 01:19:22.740
and in terms of adjusting
anxiety for its daytime use.

01:19:22.740 --> 01:19:23.573
What I'm talking about

01:19:23.573 --> 01:19:26.070
is taking 900 milligrams of myo-inositol

01:19:26.070 --> 01:19:28.170
also 30 to 60 minutes before sleep

01:19:28.170 --> 01:19:29.430
along with the standard sleep stack

01:19:29.430 --> 01:19:31.500
and I found that to be
immensely beneficial.

01:19:31.500 --> 01:19:34.170
I also noticed that it
has a pretty long tail

01:19:34.170 --> 01:19:37.230
of anxiety suppression throughout the day.

01:19:37.230 --> 01:19:39.660
And I'm not somebody who
suffers from anxiety,

01:19:39.660 --> 01:19:41.760
but I have to say it just has led me

01:19:41.760 --> 01:19:43.530
to feel a bit calmer throughout the day,

01:19:43.530 --> 01:19:45.450
and I don't really know how to say this

01:19:45.450 --> 01:19:46.560
except in subjective terms,

01:19:46.560 --> 01:19:48.540
to feel a bit more buffered against

01:19:48.540 --> 01:19:50.430
or resilient against stress events.

01:19:50.430 --> 01:19:52.590
And if you look at the
literature on inositol

01:19:52.590 --> 01:19:53.490
and its interactions

01:19:53.490 --> 01:19:56.190
with the serotonin
system and other systems,

01:19:56.190 --> 01:19:58.320
that all makes sense as to
why that would be the case.

01:19:58.320 --> 01:20:00.420
So we'll provide links to
our so-called sleep kit,

01:20:00.420 --> 01:20:02.250
which is part of our
Neural Network Newsletter.

01:20:02.250 --> 01:20:03.360
It's a zero-cost newsletter

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:05.820
where you can access this
information about supplements

01:20:05.820 --> 01:20:08.610
and other behavioral tools
for sleep in list form.

01:20:08.610 --> 01:20:10.440
But that sleep kit doesn't include

01:20:10.440 --> 01:20:11.460
some of the newer information

01:20:11.460 --> 01:20:12.720
that I've provided this episode,

01:20:12.720 --> 01:20:15.210
in particular, the
information about inositol

01:20:15.210 --> 01:20:16.043
and what I'm finding

01:20:16.043 --> 01:20:19.800
to be the very beneficial use of inositol

01:20:19.800 --> 01:20:22.230
for the ability to fall back asleep

01:20:22.230 --> 01:20:23.580
after waking up in the
middle of the night,

01:20:23.580 --> 01:20:25.650
which is something that a
lot of people struggle with.

01:20:25.650 --> 01:20:28.320
Now, that's supplementation
for falling and staying asleep,

01:20:28.320 --> 01:20:31.530
but we can return to the behavioral tools

01:20:31.530 --> 01:20:33.840
also as powerful levers and tools

01:20:33.840 --> 01:20:36.540
for falling asleep and
getting back to sleep.

01:20:36.540 --> 01:20:40.320
And again, we look to
NSDR, non-sleep deep rest,

01:20:40.320 --> 01:20:42.870
or the Reveri app as a way to do that.

01:20:42.870 --> 01:20:43.703
As I mentioned earlier,

01:20:43.703 --> 01:20:46.830
the Reveri app has been
developed on the basis

01:20:46.830 --> 01:20:49.230
of really high-quality,
peer-reviewed research,

01:20:49.230 --> 01:20:51.390
both clinical and non-clinical,

01:20:51.390 --> 01:20:52.860
by my colleague, David Spiegel,

01:20:52.860 --> 01:20:55.140
who's our associate chair
of psychiatry at Stanford.

01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:56.550
It's a wonderful tool.

01:20:56.550 --> 01:21:00.390
It does carry a cost after
the initial seven-day trial.

01:21:00.390 --> 01:21:02.522
I can tell you what the cost on that is

01:21:02.522 --> 01:21:03.970
so you can get a sense

01:21:04.980 --> 01:21:07.590
because I do realize that
anything that carries a cost,

01:21:07.590 --> 01:21:09.420
for some people, it won't be accessible.

01:21:09.420 --> 01:21:11.010
Right now, Reveri,

01:21:11.010 --> 01:21:11.910
and I should just mention,

01:21:11.910 --> 01:21:13.170
they didn't pay us for an ad read.

01:21:13.170 --> 01:21:14.670
I'm just telling you what they told me

01:21:14.670 --> 01:21:19.380
so that I can accurately report
what it costs to use Reveri.

01:21:19.380 --> 01:21:21.030
They have a monthly subscription

01:21:21.030 --> 01:21:23.220
to use the Reveri app at 14.99.

01:21:23.220 --> 01:21:25.050
You do get the seven-day free trial.

01:21:25.050 --> 01:21:28.170
They have a yearly subscription of 99.99

01:21:28.170 --> 01:21:29.250
with a seven-day free trial.

01:21:29.250 --> 01:21:31.410
And they have a lifetime
purchase, one-time purchase,

01:21:31.410 --> 01:21:33.300
of 249 with no trial.

01:21:33.300 --> 01:21:36.120
It right now is only available
for Apple, not for Android,

01:21:36.120 --> 01:21:38.190
but they are, yes, going
to have it available

01:21:38.190 --> 01:21:39.570
for Android soon.

01:21:39.570 --> 01:21:41.340
There's a signup list there.

01:21:41.340 --> 01:21:45.360
I should mention that while
the cost might seem high,

01:21:45.360 --> 01:21:47.780
if you compare that cost
to, say, supplements,

01:21:47.780 --> 01:21:50.670
or you compare that cost to a
poor night's sleep over time,

01:21:50.670 --> 01:21:54.420
the cost, at least to me,
seems somewhat modest,

01:21:54.420 --> 01:21:56.340
certainly within range
for a number of people.

01:21:56.340 --> 01:21:59.040
But I acknowledge not within
range for other people,

01:21:59.040 --> 01:22:02.880
which is why I also want
to point to zero-cost tools

01:22:02.880 --> 01:22:06.120
and the zero-cost tool for
getting asleep, staying asleep,

01:22:06.120 --> 01:22:07.560
and falling back asleep

01:22:07.560 --> 01:22:09.660
is going to be NSDR.

01:22:09.660 --> 01:22:11.550
We'll put a link to a
non-sleep deep rest protocol

01:22:11.550 --> 01:22:12.570
that's available on YouTube,

01:22:12.570 --> 01:22:14.400
so available to anybody zero cost

01:22:14.400 --> 01:22:16.830
provided you have a internet connection.

01:22:16.830 --> 01:22:17.790
Again, dim the screen

01:22:17.790 --> 01:22:19.290
if you're going to turn
that on late at night.

01:22:19.290 --> 01:22:22.680
And there are a number of
other yoga nidra scripts

01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:25.620
and apps and sources around the internet,

01:22:25.620 --> 01:22:26.460
in particular, on YouTube,

01:22:26.460 --> 01:22:28.350
that are zero cost that you could use

01:22:28.350 --> 01:22:30.660
if the Reveri app is
outside your price range

01:22:30.660 --> 01:22:34.050
or is not preferable to you, et cetera.

01:22:34.050 --> 01:22:35.430
When I wake up in the middle of the night,

01:22:35.430 --> 01:22:36.570
it's usually to use the restroom.

01:22:36.570 --> 01:22:37.403
I'll go use the restroom.

01:22:37.403 --> 01:22:38.880
I'll keep the lights as dim as possible.

01:22:38.880 --> 01:22:40.260
I'll get back into bed.

01:22:40.260 --> 01:22:42.390
And if I find that it's
easy to fall asleep,

01:22:42.390 --> 01:22:43.530
great, I'm asleep.

01:22:43.530 --> 01:22:46.560
And if not, then I will
generally plug in the Reveri app.

01:22:46.560 --> 01:22:48.701
They have a fall back asleep hypnosis,

01:22:48.701 --> 01:22:51.690
and 99 times out of a hundred,

01:22:51.690 --> 01:22:53.160
I'm back asleep within minutes

01:22:53.160 --> 01:22:54.660
and I don't wake up until morning.

01:22:54.660 --> 01:22:57.120
Now, very briefly, I just
want to touch on some tools

01:22:57.120 --> 01:22:59.220
that are very commonly used
by many people out there,

01:22:59.220 --> 01:23:00.060
and believe it or not,

01:23:00.060 --> 01:23:03.030
there is peer-reviewed science
on things like eye masks.

01:23:03.030 --> 01:23:05.970
Do eye masks improve your
ability to stay asleep?

01:23:05.970 --> 01:23:07.440
And indeed, they do,

01:23:07.440 --> 01:23:09.150
provided they are not too tight

01:23:09.150 --> 01:23:11.880
and provided that the room is cool enough.

01:23:11.880 --> 01:23:12.713
Why?

01:23:12.713 --> 01:23:14.550
Well, eye masks cover the
upper half of your face,

01:23:14.550 --> 01:23:17.610
which is where glabrous skin is localized.

01:23:17.610 --> 01:23:19.290
Remember, palms of the
hands, bottoms of the feet,

01:23:19.290 --> 01:23:21.030
glabrous skin on the face.

01:23:21.030 --> 01:23:23.700
So a lot of people who
wear eye masks will wake up

01:23:23.700 --> 01:23:25.620
because they're too warm
if the room is too warm.

01:23:25.620 --> 01:23:28.650
So if you're going to use an
eye mask to keep light out,

01:23:28.650 --> 01:23:30.120
definitely make sure the room

01:23:30.120 --> 01:23:32.700
and your sleeping environment
and your bed are cool enough

01:23:32.700 --> 01:23:34.320
in order for you to stay asleep.

01:23:34.320 --> 01:23:36.780
In addition, I get a lot of
questions about earplugs.

01:23:36.780 --> 01:23:38.070
Here's the deal with earplugs.

01:23:38.070 --> 01:23:39.990
Some people find that
earplugs are very beneficial

01:23:39.990 --> 01:23:43.830
because, of course, they
prevent the entrance of sound

01:23:43.830 --> 01:23:45.720
into the ear that could wake us up.

01:23:45.720 --> 01:23:47.640
But some people find that the sound

01:23:47.640 --> 01:23:50.820
of their own beating of their
own heart can be disruptive

01:23:50.820 --> 01:23:52.770
and they get a sort of
humming in their head

01:23:52.770 --> 01:23:54.540
when they have those earplugs in.

01:23:54.540 --> 01:23:55.470
I'm one such person.

01:23:55.470 --> 01:23:56.760
Although, I have family members

01:23:56.760 --> 01:23:58.470
that like using earplugs when they sleep.

01:23:58.470 --> 01:23:59.670
So it's really up to you.

01:23:59.670 --> 01:24:02.250
You have to see whether
or not those earplugs help

01:24:02.250 --> 01:24:03.660
or disrupt your sleep.

01:24:03.660 --> 01:24:05.250
For me, they're no good.

01:24:05.250 --> 01:24:06.930
For some people, they really enjoy them.

01:24:06.930 --> 01:24:08.220
I don't use an eye mask

01:24:08.220 --> 01:24:10.010
unless I'm sleeping in a
really bright environment

01:24:10.010 --> 01:24:12.710
or I need to sleep on a plane
and things of that sort.

01:24:13.650 --> 01:24:14.563
Other tools that I'll just mention

01:24:14.563 --> 01:24:17.640
that have peer-reviewed
research to support them.

01:24:17.640 --> 01:24:19.500
Elevating your feet either with a pillow

01:24:19.500 --> 01:24:21.600
or by elevating the end of your bed

01:24:21.600 --> 01:24:23.340
by about three to five degrees

01:24:23.340 --> 01:24:26.370
can be really beneficial for
increasing the depth of sleep

01:24:26.370 --> 01:24:28.440
because of the so-called
glymphatic washout.

01:24:28.440 --> 01:24:30.780
This is the movement of
and circulation of fluids

01:24:30.780 --> 01:24:33.090
in your brain at night

01:24:33.090 --> 01:24:34.530
that lead to more wakefulness

01:24:34.530 --> 01:24:36.450
and actually can improve
cognitive function

01:24:36.450 --> 01:24:39.270
and a number of other things
related to brain health.

01:24:39.270 --> 01:24:40.710
There's one caveat to that.

01:24:40.710 --> 01:24:43.050
For people that suffer from acid reflux,

01:24:43.050 --> 01:24:45.960
having your ankles
elevated above your chest

01:24:45.960 --> 01:24:47.610
or above your heart in
the middle of the night

01:24:47.610 --> 01:24:49.890
can actually exacerbate that acid reflux.

01:24:49.890 --> 01:24:51.030
You want to do the opposite.

01:24:51.030 --> 01:24:54.090
You want to actually elevate
the head side of your bed

01:24:54.090 --> 01:24:55.560
by about three to five degrees.

01:24:55.560 --> 01:24:58.080
Now, one of the common
causes of sleep disruption

01:24:58.080 --> 01:25:00.900
that has tremendously detrimental effects

01:25:00.900 --> 01:25:02.520
is so-called sleep apnea.

01:25:02.520 --> 01:25:05.160
So this is basically bouts of suffocation

01:25:05.160 --> 01:25:07.350
or lack of oxygenation during sleep.

01:25:07.350 --> 01:25:08.910
This is particularly the case

01:25:08.910 --> 01:25:10.500
for people that are very heavyset,

01:25:10.500 --> 01:25:12.060
and that heavyset could be from obesity,

01:25:12.060 --> 01:25:14.550
it could also be heavyset
from having too much muscle.

01:25:14.550 --> 01:25:17.010
A lot of people who are
carrying too much muscle

01:25:17.010 --> 01:25:19.110
will actually have sleep
apnea without realizing it.

01:25:19.110 --> 01:25:21.270
Sleep apnea is actually very dangerous.

01:25:21.270 --> 01:25:23.820
It's associated with a number
of cardiovascular issues.

01:25:23.820 --> 01:25:25.410
It's associated with sexual dysfunction.

01:25:25.410 --> 01:25:27.780
It's associated with
issues with cognition.

01:25:27.780 --> 01:25:28.740
Sleep apnea is bad.

01:25:28.740 --> 01:25:30.780
A lot of people will have to use the PAP,

01:25:30.780 --> 01:25:32.610
which is a, it's a device.

01:25:32.610 --> 01:25:36.240
It looks like a sort of like
a snorkel mask or a dive mask.

01:25:36.240 --> 01:25:40.140
It's a whole apparatus that
people will go to sleep with.

01:25:40.140 --> 01:25:43.650
However, many people can relieve
themselves of sleep apnea

01:25:43.650 --> 01:25:44.760
provided it's not too serious

01:25:44.760 --> 01:25:46.500
and can sleep much better,

01:25:46.500 --> 01:25:48.360
in fact, I think all people
can sleep much better,

01:25:48.360 --> 01:25:49.770
if they train themselves

01:25:49.770 --> 01:25:52.260
to be nose breathers while they sleep.

01:25:52.260 --> 01:25:53.880
There are a lot of reasons
to be a nose breather

01:25:53.880 --> 01:25:56.940
unless you are breathing
very hard due to exercise

01:25:56.940 --> 01:25:58.740
or talking or eating.

01:25:58.740 --> 01:26:00.997
That was all covered
in James Nestor's book,

01:26:00.997 --> 01:26:02.910
"Breath: The New Science of a Lost Art."

01:26:02.910 --> 01:26:04.830
It's been covered in a
number of different podcasts.

01:26:04.830 --> 01:26:07.470
We've talked about it
on this podcast as well.

01:26:07.470 --> 01:26:08.880
It's a good idea to be a nose breather

01:26:08.880 --> 01:26:10.470
unless you need to mouth breathe.

01:26:10.470 --> 01:26:12.990
And it's a great idea, it's a superb idea,

01:26:12.990 --> 01:26:15.990
to be a nose breather in sleep.

01:26:15.990 --> 01:26:17.600
And one way to really get good at that

01:26:17.600 --> 01:26:19.080
is to take a little bit of medical tape

01:26:19.080 --> 01:26:21.360
and to tape your mouth
shut before going to sleep.

01:26:21.360 --> 01:26:22.193
You heard me right.

01:26:22.193 --> 01:26:23.820
Put some medical tape over your mouth

01:26:23.820 --> 01:26:26.070
and force yourself to
nose breathe during sleep.

01:26:26.070 --> 01:26:28.830
It also prevents snoring in most cases.

01:26:28.830 --> 01:26:31.650
Really offsets sleep apnea.

01:26:31.650 --> 01:26:34.890
Sleep apnea, again, being a
very serious health concern.

01:26:34.890 --> 01:26:36.780
I should also mention as a tool

01:26:36.780 --> 01:26:39.930
that if you have a hard time
being a nose breather in sleep,

01:26:39.930 --> 01:26:42.480
you can try doing your
cardiovascular exercise,

01:26:42.480 --> 01:26:45.270
at least the lower-intensity
cardiovascular exercise,

01:26:45.270 --> 01:26:47.490
through purely nasal breathing.

01:26:47.490 --> 01:26:49.950
And one way to do that, again,
is to tape your mouth shut

01:26:49.950 --> 01:26:51.180
or put a gulp of water in your mouth,

01:26:51.180 --> 01:26:54.330
but don't actually swallow
that mouth full of water,

01:26:54.330 --> 01:26:56.040
or to use a mouthpiece

01:26:56.040 --> 01:26:58.170
or just deliberately
keep your mouth closed

01:26:58.170 --> 01:27:00.000
and insist on breathing through your nose.

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:00.833
Most people find

01:27:00.833 --> 01:27:02.940
that when they start doing
cardiovascular exercise that way,

01:27:02.940 --> 01:27:04.950
it's really challenging at first,

01:27:04.950 --> 01:27:07.440
but over time, they
actually can feel quite calm

01:27:07.440 --> 01:27:09.990
and still can generate
a lot of physical effort

01:27:09.990 --> 01:27:11.390
purely using nose breathing.

01:27:12.240 --> 01:27:13.830
The reason that doing nose breathing

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:15.270
during cardiovascular exercise

01:27:15.270 --> 01:27:17.640
translates to being a
nose breather during sleep

01:27:17.640 --> 01:27:21.030
is that your sinuses actually
can dilate, they're plastic,

01:27:21.030 --> 01:27:21.863
and over time,

01:27:21.863 --> 01:27:25.230
plastic meaning they're malleable that is,

01:27:25.230 --> 01:27:26.670
and they can become wider.

01:27:26.670 --> 01:27:28.530
You're not going to get giant
nostrils. Don't worry about it.

01:27:28.530 --> 01:27:31.380
Your airways within your skull,

01:27:31.380 --> 01:27:33.060
'cause that's what the sinuses really are,

01:27:33.060 --> 01:27:34.830
these little passages within the skull,

01:27:34.830 --> 01:27:36.750
and, of course, within the nasal passages,

01:27:36.750 --> 01:27:38.910
will dilate and will allow you

01:27:38.910 --> 01:27:40.260
to breathe more easily through your nose.

01:27:40.260 --> 01:27:41.370
But for those of you

01:27:41.370 --> 01:27:42.960
that are waking up in
the middle of the night

01:27:42.960 --> 01:27:44.910
breathing on your back [breathing heavily]

01:27:44.910 --> 01:27:46.200
or your partner is telling you that

01:27:46.200 --> 01:27:47.300
or other people are telling you that

01:27:47.300 --> 01:27:48.540
or you're that person on the plane

01:27:48.540 --> 01:27:50.220
with your mouth hanging open and drooling

01:27:50.220 --> 01:27:51.870
and your mouth breathing,

01:27:51.870 --> 01:27:53.970
terrible, terrible,
terrible for health reasons

01:27:53.970 --> 01:27:54.810
and other reasons,

01:27:54.810 --> 01:27:56.490
put some a medical tape over your mouth,

01:27:56.490 --> 01:27:58.290
learn to be a nose breather during sleep.

01:27:58.290 --> 01:27:59.520
Your sleep will improve

01:27:59.520 --> 01:28:01.230
and your daytime feelings

01:28:01.230 --> 01:28:02.910
of wakefulness and focus will improve,

01:28:02.910 --> 01:28:05.130
your cardiovascular health will improve,

01:28:05.130 --> 01:28:06.210
and on and on and on.

01:28:06.210 --> 01:28:08.940
So now we've largely covered
the tools that one could use

01:28:08.940 --> 01:28:10.740
to get and stay asleep,

01:28:10.740 --> 01:28:11.820
and we talked about exercise,

01:28:11.820 --> 01:28:12.750
we talked about temperature,

01:28:12.750 --> 01:28:14.580
we talked about supplements,

01:28:14.580 --> 01:28:15.510
and we talked about, of course,

01:28:15.510 --> 01:28:17.220
keeping the sleeping environment

01:28:17.220 --> 01:28:20.820
both cool and as dark as possible.

01:28:20.820 --> 01:28:24.360
I do want to mention a
couple of broad contour tools

01:28:24.360 --> 01:28:26.070
that will impact your ability

01:28:26.070 --> 01:28:28.590
to sleep really well on a consistent basis

01:28:28.590 --> 01:28:30.780
and the one that impacts
the most number of people

01:28:30.780 --> 01:28:33.000
is weekends.

01:28:33.000 --> 01:28:35.640
Turns out that most
everybody feels the impulse

01:28:35.640 --> 01:28:36.900
to sleep in on the weekend,

01:28:36.900 --> 01:28:38.970
especially if they've been
out late the night before.

01:28:38.970 --> 01:28:40.740
However, the data show

01:28:40.740 --> 01:28:44.280
that keeping relatively
consistent sleep and wake times

01:28:44.280 --> 01:28:45.630
is really going to enhance

01:28:45.630 --> 01:28:47.880
the quality and depth of your sleep.

01:28:47.880 --> 01:28:50.010
So if you stay out late one night,

01:28:50.010 --> 01:28:51.300
sure, you might allow yourself

01:28:51.300 --> 01:28:52.920
to sleep in an extra hour or so,

01:28:52.920 --> 01:28:56.790
but you should really try
to avoid sleeping in longer

01:28:56.790 --> 01:29:00.270
than an hour beyond your
normal wake-up time.

01:29:00.270 --> 01:29:01.103
That's right.

01:29:01.103 --> 01:29:02.640
If you normally get eight hours of sleep

01:29:02.640 --> 01:29:04.800
and you wake up at 7:00 a.m.,

01:29:04.800 --> 01:29:08.250
probably okay to wake up
at 8:00 a.m. on the weekend

01:29:08.250 --> 01:29:10.830
or after a night out the night before,

01:29:10.830 --> 01:29:13.140
but try not to sleep until 11 or noon

01:29:13.140 --> 01:29:15.060
thinking that you're going
to catch up on your sleep

01:29:15.060 --> 01:29:17.430
or that's better than waking
up at a consistent time.

01:29:17.430 --> 01:29:19.470
It would be better to wake
up at a consistent time

01:29:19.470 --> 01:29:20.640
plus or minus an hour

01:29:20.640 --> 01:29:22.080
and get a nap in the afternoon

01:29:22.080 --> 01:29:25.140
provided that nap, again, isn't too long.

01:29:25.140 --> 01:29:26.520
And the other tool

01:29:26.520 --> 01:29:28.860
that relates to nights that
you stayed out too late

01:29:28.860 --> 01:29:31.770
or that you feel like you
want to sleep in a bit more

01:29:31.770 --> 01:29:32.603
in the morning

01:29:32.603 --> 01:29:36.150
is if you are going to wake
up at your consistent time.

01:29:36.150 --> 01:29:39.180
So for example, normally
you go to bed at 10

01:29:39.180 --> 01:29:40.230
and you wake up at six.

01:29:40.230 --> 01:29:42.330
Let's say that's your schedule.

01:29:42.330 --> 01:29:44.250
And you end up staying up late one night

01:29:44.250 --> 01:29:46.829
until midnight or one for whatever reason

01:29:46.829 --> 01:29:49.320
and the next morning you wake up at seven

01:29:49.320 --> 01:29:50.643
and you're still groggy.

01:29:51.540 --> 01:29:54.150
In that case, you absolutely want to wait

01:29:54.150 --> 01:29:57.480
to ingest caffeine 90 to
120 minutes after waking.

01:29:57.480 --> 01:29:59.540
You really do because there are good data

01:29:59.540 --> 01:30:02.913
to support the fact that
caffeine can disrupt sleep.

01:30:03.810 --> 01:30:05.010
Yes, that's obvious.

01:30:05.010 --> 01:30:06.990
Caffeine especially disrupts sleep

01:30:06.990 --> 01:30:08.910
if you take it too late in the day.

01:30:08.910 --> 01:30:11.520
That's very obvious as to
why that would be the case.

01:30:11.520 --> 01:30:14.460
But caffeine especially disrupts

01:30:14.460 --> 01:30:16.650
what's called compensatory sleep.

01:30:16.650 --> 01:30:19.560
So if you start changing your waking time

01:30:19.560 --> 01:30:20.940
and your to sleep time

01:30:20.940 --> 01:30:23.190
and you start using additional caffeine

01:30:23.190 --> 01:30:25.320
to offset the sleepiness
that you're experiencing

01:30:25.320 --> 01:30:26.820
because of those late nights out,

01:30:26.820 --> 01:30:28.920
well, that's when you
really start to disrupt

01:30:28.920 --> 01:30:30.360
not just your nighttime sleep,

01:30:30.360 --> 01:30:32.610
but your daytime compensatory
sleep, so those naps.

01:30:32.610 --> 01:30:36.210
You also are disrupting the
total architecture of sleep

01:30:36.210 --> 01:30:37.410
in the early morning hours.

01:30:37.410 --> 01:30:40.230
There's a lot of great science
that's been put to this,

01:30:40.230 --> 01:30:42.180
or that's emerged from this, I should say.

01:30:42.180 --> 01:30:45.390
So try and keep those sleep-wake
times relatively constant

01:30:45.390 --> 01:30:46.470
plus or minus an hour,

01:30:46.470 --> 01:30:50.430
and try as much as you can
to delay that caffeine intake

01:30:50.430 --> 01:30:52.980
90 to 120 minutes after waking every day,

01:30:52.980 --> 01:30:55.230
but especially on days where you wake up

01:30:55.230 --> 01:30:56.610
and you feel you haven't
gotten enough sleep.

01:30:56.610 --> 01:30:58.170
In that case, I highly recommend

01:30:58.170 --> 01:31:00.780
you just use NSDR or the Reveri app

01:31:00.780 --> 01:31:03.510
or some other form of deep relaxation

01:31:03.510 --> 01:31:05.710
to try and compensate
for the lack of sleep,

01:31:06.690 --> 01:31:10.590
knowing, of course, that
there's no complete compensation

01:31:10.590 --> 01:31:11.423
for lack of sleep.

01:31:11.423 --> 01:31:12.630
There are just things that we can do

01:31:12.630 --> 01:31:14.820
to partially offset lack of sleep.

01:31:14.820 --> 01:31:17.280
Now, a couple of final
points and additional tools

01:31:17.280 --> 01:31:19.770
that I think are going to
be useful to everybody,

01:31:19.770 --> 01:31:22.710
in particular, people
who have young children

01:31:22.710 --> 01:31:25.470
or are following a shift work schedule

01:31:25.470 --> 01:31:28.560
or who are experiencing jet lag.

01:31:28.560 --> 01:31:31.260
Keep in mind, jet lag
can be due to travel,

01:31:31.260 --> 01:31:32.093
which is obvious,

01:31:32.093 --> 01:31:33.570
but jet lag can also be due

01:31:33.570 --> 01:31:35.670
to getting woken up in the
middle of the night, right?

01:31:35.670 --> 01:31:36.720
Your body doesn't know the difference

01:31:36.720 --> 01:31:38.850
between flying to a new time zone

01:31:38.850 --> 01:31:41.010
and getting woken up in
the middle of the night.

01:31:41.010 --> 01:31:42.260
The tool that I'd like to offer you

01:31:42.260 --> 01:31:45.240
is an understanding of something
called temperature minimum.

01:31:45.240 --> 01:31:46.950
And I'm going to make
this as simple as possible

01:31:46.950 --> 01:31:49.080
and I'm confident that
everyone can understand this

01:31:49.080 --> 01:31:51.990
even if you don't have
any science background.

01:31:51.990 --> 01:31:54.450
Here's the question you
need to ask yourself.

01:31:54.450 --> 01:31:58.200
What is your typical wake-up time, okay?

01:31:58.200 --> 01:31:59.310
What's your typical wake-up time?

01:31:59.310 --> 01:32:01.950
If for you, your typical
wake-up time is 7:00 a.m.

01:32:01.950 --> 01:32:03.303
plus or minus half an hour,

01:32:04.170 --> 01:32:05.130
and that could be 7:00 a.m.

01:32:05.130 --> 01:32:06.450
because you set an alarm clock

01:32:06.450 --> 01:32:07.380
or it could be 7:00 a.m.

01:32:07.380 --> 01:32:10.050
because you naturally wake up
at 7:00 a.m., doesn't matter,

01:32:10.050 --> 01:32:13.710
if your typical wake-up
time most days is 7:00 a.m.,

01:32:13.710 --> 01:32:16.590
well, then your temperature
minimum is 5:00 a.m.

01:32:16.590 --> 01:32:18.960
That's right, your temperature
minimum is not a temperature.

01:32:18.960 --> 01:32:21.873
It's a time within your 24-hour cycle.

01:32:22.890 --> 01:32:26.130
Approximately two hours before
your typical wake-up time,

01:32:26.130 --> 01:32:28.560
your body is at its lowest temperature

01:32:28.560 --> 01:32:31.050
that it will ever be in the 24-hour cycle.

01:32:31.050 --> 01:32:33.450
That's why it's called
your temperature minimum.

01:32:33.450 --> 01:32:35.670
Here's what you need to know
about your temperature minimum.

01:32:35.670 --> 01:32:40.620
If you view bright light,
exercise, or drink caffeine

01:32:40.620 --> 01:32:42.240
or all of the above

01:32:42.240 --> 01:32:47.240
in the two to four hours before
your temperature minimum,

01:32:47.400 --> 01:32:49.560
that will delay your clock.

01:32:49.560 --> 01:32:51.420
What that means when
I say delay your clock

01:32:51.420 --> 01:32:54.120
is it will make you want
to go to sleep later

01:32:54.120 --> 01:32:56.790
and wake up later the next night, okay?

01:32:56.790 --> 01:32:58.770
So let's run this exercise for you,

01:32:58.770 --> 01:33:02.040
the person waking up at 7:00
a.m. on a regular basis,

01:33:02.040 --> 01:33:04.470
I can predict with almost certainty

01:33:04.470 --> 01:33:07.410
that your body is going to
be at its lowest temperature

01:33:07.410 --> 01:33:08.700
at 5:00 a.m..

01:33:08.700 --> 01:33:09.960
So what that means

01:33:09.960 --> 01:33:14.460
is that if you get up at
3:00 a.m. or at 4:00 a.m.

01:33:14.460 --> 01:33:16.620
and you flip on bright
lights in your house

01:33:16.620 --> 01:33:17.820
or in your bathroom

01:33:17.820 --> 01:33:18.990
or you have a cup of coffee

01:33:18.990 --> 01:33:20.310
or you do any kind of exercise

01:33:20.310 --> 01:33:22.770
or you get up and head to the airport,

01:33:22.770 --> 01:33:25.260
the mechanisms in your brain and body

01:33:25.260 --> 01:33:26.790
that control timing of sleep

01:33:26.790 --> 01:33:28.200
and timing of waking will shift.

01:33:28.200 --> 01:33:29.340
They will delay.

01:33:29.340 --> 01:33:32.490
It's as if you put your clock
on hold for a little while

01:33:32.490 --> 01:33:34.170
and then let it start again, okay?

01:33:34.170 --> 01:33:35.730
That's the simplest way I can describe it.

01:33:35.730 --> 01:33:38.670
And you will tend to
want to go to sleep later

01:33:38.670 --> 01:33:41.490
and wake up later the following night.

01:33:41.490 --> 01:33:43.650
Now, the opposite is true

01:33:43.650 --> 01:33:47.040
if you view bright light, drink caffeine,

01:33:47.040 --> 01:33:50.040
or exercise or socialize, I should say,

01:33:50.040 --> 01:33:53.760
in the hours immediately after
your temperature minimum.

01:33:53.760 --> 01:33:55.620
So for you in this example,

01:33:55.620 --> 01:33:57.450
the person who's waking up at 7:00 a.m.,

01:33:57.450 --> 01:33:59.280
your temperature minimum is 5:00 a.m.,

01:33:59.280 --> 01:34:01.110
if you view bright light, exercise,

01:34:01.110 --> 01:34:04.984
maybe have a snack, maybe
not, or socialize, move about,

01:34:04.984 --> 01:34:08.580
at 5:30 or 6:00 a.m. or 7:00 a.m.

01:34:08.580 --> 01:34:12.330
That will tend to phase
advance your clock.

01:34:12.330 --> 01:34:14.400
It will tend to basically make you

01:34:14.400 --> 01:34:17.190
want to go to bed earlier
and wake up earlier

01:34:17.190 --> 01:34:18.150
the following night.

01:34:18.150 --> 01:34:19.170
Now, I used this example

01:34:19.170 --> 01:34:21.390
of a person who wakes up
typically at 7:00 a.m.

01:34:21.390 --> 01:34:22.980
whose temperature minimum is 5:00 a.m.,

01:34:22.980 --> 01:34:25.710
but, of course, you need
to adjust that for yourself

01:34:25.710 --> 01:34:26.607
if you're somebody who
wakes up at 9:00 a.m.

01:34:26.607 --> 01:34:28.710
or at 5:00 a.m. et cetera.

01:34:28.710 --> 01:34:30.060
Why do I offer this as a tool?

01:34:30.060 --> 01:34:32.550
Well, this is an immensely powerful tool

01:34:32.550 --> 01:34:34.740
if, for instance, you
are headed to a time zone

01:34:34.740 --> 01:34:36.030
where you need to go to bed earlier

01:34:36.030 --> 01:34:38.730
and wake up earlier once you
arrive in that time zone.

01:34:38.730 --> 01:34:41.520
What it means is in the day
or two before you leave,

01:34:41.520 --> 01:34:46.500
you can force yourself to
exercise, drink caffeine,

01:34:46.500 --> 01:34:48.990
maybe even to eat a meal
early in the morning,

01:34:48.990 --> 01:34:50.307
or maybe you still fast
early in the morning

01:34:50.307 --> 01:34:51.750
and that's really up to you,

01:34:51.750 --> 01:34:54.540
but you force yourself
to do the activities

01:34:54.540 --> 01:34:57.390
that are going to phase
advance your clock.

01:34:57.390 --> 01:34:59.280
Whereas if you're traveling to a time zone

01:34:59.280 --> 01:35:02.130
where you are going to need
to go to sleep much later

01:35:02.130 --> 01:35:05.040
and you're going to need
to wake up much later

01:35:05.040 --> 01:35:06.330
or even a little bit later,

01:35:06.330 --> 01:35:07.440
you can do those things

01:35:07.440 --> 01:35:10.740
in the hours prior to
your temperature minimum.

01:35:10.740 --> 01:35:12.510
Now, for those of you
that work shift work,

01:35:12.510 --> 01:35:14.340
this can be especially useful,

01:35:14.340 --> 01:35:17.520
but I want to say a couple
of things about shift work.

01:35:17.520 --> 01:35:19.650
There are a lot of details
about shift work and jet lag

01:35:19.650 --> 01:35:20.850
in an episode that I did

01:35:20.850 --> 01:35:22.860
specifically about jet lag and shift work.

01:35:22.860 --> 01:35:24.150
So for the deep dive, go there,

01:35:24.150 --> 01:35:26.523
but suffice to say this for now,

01:35:27.570 --> 01:35:29.160
if you are going to do shift work,

01:35:29.160 --> 01:35:32.040
try to stay on the same shift
for two weeks at a time.

01:35:32.040 --> 01:35:34.350
It's very detrimental to brain and body,

01:35:34.350 --> 01:35:37.740
it can even be horrifically challenging

01:35:37.740 --> 01:35:39.510
for your brain and body
in a number of ways,

01:35:39.510 --> 01:35:42.420
if you are switching on
the so-called swing shift,

01:35:42.420 --> 01:35:43.890
you know, you're working
three days the night shift,

01:35:43.890 --> 01:35:44.723
three days the day shift,

01:35:44.723 --> 01:35:45.990
three days the night
shift, three days the...

01:35:45.990 --> 01:35:49.020
Try and stay on the same
schedule as much as possible.

01:35:49.020 --> 01:35:50.940
And I should say for everybody,

01:35:50.940 --> 01:35:54.840
people who are jet lagged and
engaged in shift work or not,

01:35:54.840 --> 01:35:55.920
but just for everybody,

01:35:55.920 --> 01:35:59.700
if you need to be awake in
the middle of your sleep cycle

01:35:59.700 --> 01:36:02.220
and it's not just a quick
departure to the bathroom

01:36:02.220 --> 01:36:03.480
and back to bed,

01:36:03.480 --> 01:36:04.560
but you really need to be awake,

01:36:04.560 --> 01:36:05.790
you know, you're feeding a baby

01:36:05.790 --> 01:36:08.160
or you're taking care of a loved one

01:36:08.160 --> 01:36:09.570
or you need to do
something that's critical

01:36:09.570 --> 01:36:10.403
or you need to work,

01:36:10.403 --> 01:36:13.020
if possible, use red light, okay?

01:36:13.020 --> 01:36:15.390
Now, for shift workers
who really are trying

01:36:15.390 --> 01:36:17.490
to stay awake all night and sleep all day,

01:36:17.490 --> 01:36:18.840
this is not going to be ideal,

01:36:18.840 --> 01:36:20.610
but for people that, for instance,

01:36:20.610 --> 01:36:22.020
need to stay up really late one night

01:36:22.020 --> 01:36:24.120
or wake up especially
early, like 3:00 a.m.,

01:36:24.120 --> 01:36:26.520
to prepare for an exam that
you're just not ready for

01:36:26.520 --> 01:36:29.340
or to head to the airport, et cetera,

01:36:29.340 --> 01:36:31.080
using red light has been shown

01:36:31.080 --> 01:36:33.270
to allow people to be awake enough

01:36:33.270 --> 01:36:34.800
and obviously see what they need to see

01:36:34.800 --> 01:36:37.080
in order to perform
their activities safely

01:36:37.080 --> 01:36:40.680
but it does not seem to
disrupt the cortisol rhythm

01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:43.350
that is the healthy,
normal cortisol rhythm.

01:36:43.350 --> 01:36:45.360
Now, I realize this is
kind of an advanced tool

01:36:45.360 --> 01:36:46.920
and many people won't have access to this.

01:36:46.920 --> 01:36:50.040
There are a number of different
sources for red lights now.

01:36:50.040 --> 01:36:53.010
Companies like Joovv or KOZE light.

01:36:53.010 --> 01:36:54.120
These are different brands.

01:36:54.120 --> 01:36:55.140
I don't have any affiliation

01:36:55.140 --> 01:36:56.340
to any of these brands, I should say.

01:36:56.340 --> 01:36:57.600
There are a number of different

01:36:57.600 --> 01:37:01.230
red light bulb sources out
there and commercial sources

01:37:01.230 --> 01:37:02.700
that you can explore if you want.

01:37:02.700 --> 01:37:04.740
But understanding this temperature minimum

01:37:04.740 --> 01:37:06.150
is really powerful

01:37:06.150 --> 01:37:07.920
because it allows you
to adjust your schedule

01:37:07.920 --> 01:37:08.790
depending on travel,

01:37:08.790 --> 01:37:11.520
depending on changing work
schedules or school schedules.

01:37:11.520 --> 01:37:12.870
And if you're not a morning person,

01:37:12.870 --> 01:37:15.630
you can use the tools related
to temperature minimum

01:37:15.630 --> 01:37:18.570
to really become a
morning person over time,

01:37:18.570 --> 01:37:20.130
and it actually is pretty easy.

01:37:20.130 --> 01:37:22.350
And I talked about this
in a previous episode

01:37:22.350 --> 01:37:24.300
but I'll just mention
that there have been shown

01:37:24.300 --> 01:37:27.240
to be important, positive
effects on cognition,

01:37:27.240 --> 01:37:29.670
on even grip strength
and physical performance,

01:37:29.670 --> 01:37:32.880
for people that are early morning risers,

01:37:32.880 --> 01:37:35.430
and that's especially true for night owls

01:37:35.430 --> 01:37:38.700
that deliberately shift
themselves to become early risers.

